{!<p class="other">!}[? if year = 1930 and month = 8: The film adaptation of the controversial anti-war novel <i>All Quiet on the Western Front</i>has released in America, but has yet to arrive on our shores. ?]{!</p>!}
{!<p class="other">!}[? if flavour_8_trigger and flavour_8_trigger_time == time: Many youth and bourgeois unity organizations that initially supported the <span style="color: #D3C24D;">**[+ ddp_name +]**</span> have since distanced themselves from the party due to a sharp rebuff from the <span style="color: #C0A054;">**DVP**</span>'s [+ dvp_leader +]. These <span style="color: #C0A054;">**DVP**</span> affiliated organizations, initially frustrated by the lack of progress toward bourgeois concentration, have now shifted their stance. They condemn the founding of the <span style="color: #D3C24D;">**[+ ddp_name +]**</span>, arguing that it is incompatible with bourgeois unity, and called on the founders of the <span style="color: #D3C24D;">**[+ ddp_name +]**</span> to join the <span style="color: #C0A054;">**DVP**</span> in the creation of a truly comprehensive middle party. These events, coupled with the hostility that has arisen towards the party, have significantly dampened the initial enthusiasm surrounding its founding. ?]{!</p>!}
{!<p class="other">!}[? if flavour_9_trigger and flavour_9_trigger_time == time: The anti-Semitism and romanticism of the Young German Order have caused intense conflict with the <span style="color: #005EB8;">Jews</span> and old-line <span style="color: #D3C24D;">democrats</span> within the <span style="color: #D3C24D;">**[+ ddp_name +]**</span>. A notable example is Gustav Stolper, a <span style="color: #005EB8;">Jew</span> who faced strong attacks from the Young Germans and was almost denied a list seat, only avoiding this fate due to threats of resignation from the old-line <span style="color: #D3C24D;">democrats</span>. The conflict between the Young Germans and the <span style="color: #D3C24D;">democrats</span> is so intense that not one of the <span style="color: #D3D3A9;">**VNR**</span>'s candidates could be selected without resistance from local organizations. [? if dvp_ideology == "Right": At least the <span style="color: #D3C24D;">**[+ ddp_name +]**</span> has shown a strong stance against the <span style="color: #7A3C00;">**NSDAP**</span>, which cannot be said for the <span style="color: #C0A054;">**DVP**</span>, who remain bitter over the debacle caused by the formation.?] ?]{!</p>!}
{!<p class="both">!}[? if flavour_6_trigger and flavour_6_trigger_time == time: The lack of attention Hugenberg's <span style="color: #3E88B3;">**DNVP**</span> has devoted to attacking [? if nsdap_leader == "Hitler": Adolf Hitler?][? if nsdap_leader == "Goebbels": Joseph Goebbels?]'s <span style="color: #7A3C00;">**NSDAP**</span> is looking to be a blunder of enormous proportions. Despite the party's silence dealing with the <span style="color: #7A3C00;">Nazis</span>, the <span style="color: #7A3C00;">Nazis</span> aren't returning the favor, and have a lot to gain from the <span style="color: #3E88B3;">**DNVP**</span> attacking us on their behalf. The <span style="color: #3E88B3;">**DNVP**</span>'s goal of rallying the conservative and nationalist forces around themselves is ultimately benefitting the <span style="color: #7A3C00;">Nazis</span> more than it is helping themselves. ?]{!</p>!}
{!<p class="dnvp">!}[? if year = 1930 and month = 9 and dnvp_ideology == "Moderate": [? if dnvp_leader == "Hergt": With the <span style="color: #3E88B3;">**DNVP**</span>'s party congress just two months away, Hergt and his ruling old guard preside over a relatively stable party. However, two key issues remain contentious: Treviranus and his Young Conservatives advocate for a shift toward democracy, while the question of cooperation with the rising <span style="color: #7A3C00;">**NSDAP**</span> looms large, as the <span style="color: #7A3C00;">Nazis</span> threaten to eclipse the <span style="color: #3E88B3;">**DNVP**</span> as the dominant force on the right. ?][? if dnvp_leader == "Lambach": Over the past few months, Lambach has made increasing enemies within the <span style="color: #3E88B3;">**DNVP**</span> as he pushes his <span style="color: #6D5298;">Christian Social</span> ideals into the party platform and expands the role of workers and trade unions within the party. Many aligned with the old guard and the <span style="color: #385A38;">agrarian</span> wing now threaten to defect to the <span style="color: #003755;">**DNF**</span> if Lambach continues to sideline their interests. ?][? if dnvp_leader == "Treviranus": Treviranus has increasingly clashed with the <span style="color: #3E88B3;">**DNVP**</span>'s old guard, led by national conservatives like Westarp, over his push to embrace democracy and sideline monarchism as official party policy. Additionally, his positions on key "national questions"—especially on reparations—mark a radical departure from the <span style="color: #3E88B3;">**DNVP**</span>’s traditional stance. ?] ?]{!</p>!}
{!<p class="other">!}[? if year = 1930 and month = 9 and dvp_leader == "Scholz" and not lvp_formed and not lvp_merger_2_timer : The severe atrophy of the <span style="color: #C0A054;">**DVP**</span>'s vote share and party organizations has sparked internal opposition against their party leader Ernst Scholz, who many believe is responsible for derailing negotiations towards bourgeois concentration. Walther Schnell—the leader of one of their district organizations—is fed up with Scholz's inaction and urges him that the very fate of the German bourgeoisie depends on its consolidation into a single party. ?]{!</p>!}
{!<p class="other">!}[? if flavour_12_trigger and flavour_12_trigger_time == time: Our decision to tolerate Brüning's government, while appreciated, has become a significant liability that will severely undermine Brüning's support from the more conservative factions of Germany's bourgeois elite. This is especially true among German heavy industry, where they have redoubled efforts to foster a rapprochement between Brüning and the <span style="color: #7A3C00;">far</span>-<span style="color: #3E88B3;">right</span> parties to prevent the chancellor from becoming overly reliant on our support. Brüning's government's relationship with the capitalist economic interests that initially supported him so enthusiastically has now become increasingly strained. [? if dvp_ideology == "Right" and not lvp_formed and dvp_exist: The right-wing <span style="color: #C0A054;">**DVP**</span> is so displeased by this development that it no longer feels strong loyalty toward Brüning and his government. It’s as if the <span style="color: #C0A054;">**DVP**</span> hasn’t already toppled enough governments.?][? if lvp_ideology == "Right" and lvp_formed: The right-wing elements of the <span style="color: #FFCC00;">**LVP**</span> are displeased by this development that it no longer feels strong loyalty toward Brüning and his government. ?]  ?]{!</p>!}
{!<p class="other">!}[? if flavour_10_trigger and flavour_10_trigger_time == time : For one reason or another, the <span style="color: #D3C24D;">**[+ ddp_name +]**</span> lacks the Reichstag delegates to form a parliamentary faction and is now required to negotiate with another party to join forces. In this case, the <span style="color: #D3C24D;">**[+ ddp_name +]**</span> has decided to work with the <span style="color: #C0A054;">**DVP**</span> to establish a parliamentary faction. Surprisingly, the <span style="color: #C0A054;">**DVP**</span> was interested in accommodating with the <span style="color: #D3C24D;">**[+ ddp_name +]**</span>, [? if dvp_leader == "Scholz" and dstp_formed: but just as they were about to finalize their agreement, an angry Ernst Scholz torpedoed the negotiations, still furious about how the <span style="color: #D3C24D;">**[+ ddp_name +]**</span> disrupted his efforts toward bourgeois unity. Fortunately, the <span style="color: #D3C24D;">**[+ ddp_name +]**</span> was able to accept a few defections from the <span style="color: #b8996f;">**WP**</span> to reach the required size for a parliamentary faction.?][? if dvp_leader != "Scholz" or not dstp_formed: [? if chancellor == "Brüning" and not spd_in_government: having ceased their intrigues against Brüning's government in order to finalize the agreement.?][? if not (chancellor == "Brüning" and not spd_in_government): having put aside their grudges to finalize the agreement.?]?] ?]{!</p>!}
{!<p class="other">!}[? if flavour_11_trigger and flavour_11_trigger_time == time : For one reason or another, the <span style="color: #D3C24D;">**[+ ddp_name +]**</span> lacks the Reichstag delegates to form a parliamentary faction and is now required to negotiate with another party to join forces. In this case, the <span style="color: #D3C24D;">**[+ ddp_name +]**</span> has decided to work with the <span style="color: #C0A054;">**DVP**</span> to establish a parliamentary faction. Surprisingly, the <span style="color: #C0A054;">**DVP**</span> was interested in accommodating with the <span style="color: #D3C24D;">**[+ ddp_name +]**</span>, [? if dvp_leader == "Scholz" and dstp_formed: but just as they were about to finalize their agreement, an angry Ernst Scholz torpedoed the negotiations, still furious about how the <span style="color: #D3C24D;">**[+ ddp_name +]**</span> disrupted his efforts toward bourgeois unity. As a result of this, the <span style="color: #D3C24D;">**[+ ddp_name +]**</span> has formed an agreement with the <span style="color: #b8996f;">**WP**</span> instead, having exhausted its choices. ?][? if dvp_leader != "Scholz" or not dstp_formed: [? if chancellor == "Brüning" and not spd_in_government: having ceased their intrigues against Brüning's government in order to finalize the agreement.?][? if not (chancellor == "Brüning" and not spd_in_government): having put aside their grudges to finalize the agreement.?]?] ?]{!</p>!}
{!<p class="other">!}[? if flavour_32_trigger and flavour_32_trigger_time == time : For one reason or another, the <span style="color: #D3C24D;">**[+ ddp_name +]**</span> lacks the Reichstag delegates to form a parliamentary faction and is now required to negotiate with another party to join forces. With the bourgeois parties already grouped together, the <span style="color: #D3C24D;">**[+ ddp_name +]**</span> is forced to negotiate with them. Surprisingly, the bourgeois parties have gotten over their grudges against the party, and are open to cooperation. [? if dvp_leader == "Scholz" and dstp_formed: However, just as they were about to finalize their agreement, an angry Ernst Scholz torpedoed the negotiations, still furious about how the <span style="color: #D3C24D;">**[+ ddp_name +]**</span> disrupted his efforts toward bourgeois unity. Fortunately, the <span style="color: #D3C24D;">**[+ ddp_name +]**</span> was able to accept a few defections from the various parties within the group to reach the required size for a parliamentary faction.?][? if dvp_leader != "Scholz" or not dstp_formed: [? if chancellor == "Brüning" and not spd_in_government: They have all agreed to cease their intrigues against Brüning's government in order to finalize the agreement.?][? if not (chancellor == "Brüning" and not spd_in_government): With past disputes set aside, they've finalized the agreement.?]?] ?]{!</p>!}
{!<p class="other">!}[? if flavour_33_trigger and flavour_33_trigger_time == time : For one reason or another, the <span style="color: #D3C24D;">**[+ ddp_name +]**</span> lacks the Reichstag delegates to form a parliamentary faction and is now required to negotiate with another party to join forces. With the bourgeois parties already grouped together, the <span style="color: #D3C24D;">**[+ ddp_name +]**</span> is forced to negotiate with them. Surprisingly, the bourgeois parties have gotten over their grudges against the party, and are open to cooperation. [? if dvp_leader == "Scholz" and dstp_formed: However, just as they were about to finalize their agreement, an angry Ernst Scholz torpedoed the negotiations, still furious about how the <span style="color: #D3C24D;">**[+ ddp_name +]**</span> disrupted his efforts toward bourgeois unity. As a result of this, the <span style="color: #D3C24D;">**[+ ddp_name +]**</span> is forced to enter the [? if z_party_name != "CVP": <span style="color: #000000;">Center</span>?][? if z_party_name == "CVP": <span style="color: #000000;">**CVP**</span>?]'s parliamentary group. ?][? if dvp_leader != "Scholz" or not dstp_formed: [? if chancellor == "Brüning" and not spd_in_government: They have all agreed to cease their intrigues against Brüning's government in order to finalize the agreement.?][? if not (chancellor == "Brüning" and not spd_in_government): With past disputes set aside, they've finalized the agreement.?]?] ?]{!</p>!}
{!<p class="other">!}[? if flavour_54_trigger and flavour_54_trigger_time == time : The <span style="color: #D3C24D;">**[+ ddp_name +]**</span>, for one reason or another, lacks the necessary Reichstag delegates to form a parliamentary faction. As a result, it is forced to conclude an agreement [? if wp_r + ddp_r >= 3.6: with the <span style="color: #b8996f;">**WP**</span> and other minor bourgeois parties?][? if wp_r + ddp_r < 3.6: to enter the [? if z_party_name != "CVP": <span style="color: #000000;">Center</span>?][? if z_party_name == "CVP": <span style="color: #000000;">**CVP**</span>?]'s parliamentary group?]. The merger of the <span style="color: #C0A054;">**DVP**</span> into the <span style="color: #3E88B3;">**DNVP**</span> has effectively ruled out the possibility of a unified liberal parliamentary group. ?]{!</p>!}
{!<p class="other">!}[? if year = 1930 and month = 9 : In the small Free State of Brunswick, elections are held, resulting in [? if not brunswick_hitler: another majority for the <span style="color: #c00000;">**SPD**</span>?][? if brunswick_hitler: a victory for the bourgeois unity list and the <span style="color: #7A3C00;">**NSDAP**</span>, who enter government together despite our plurality of votes?]. [? if brunswick_hitler: The <span style="color: #7A3C00;">**NSDAP**</span> is expected to use their new position in government to grant Hitler citizenship, as he remains stateless. ?]?]{!</p>!}
{!<p class="dnvp">!}[? if year = 1930 and month = 9 : Adolf Hitler of the <span style="color: #7A3C00;">**NSDAP**</span> has taken an oath of legality, pledging to use constitutional means to gain power. This occurred during a court case involving <span style="color: #7A3C00;">Nazi</span> members and Reichswehr officers Richard Scheringer and Hanns Ludin, who were accused of plotting a national uprising by establishing <span style="color: #7A3C00;">**NSDAP**</span> cells within the Reichswehr. Under normal circumstances, Hitler's statements—tantamount to preparing for high treason—would have led to prosecution. Instead, the authorities are tolerating, even legitimizing the <span style="color: #7A3C00;">Nazi</span> agenda. ?]{!</p>!}
{!<p class="dnvp">!}[? if flavour_18_trigger and flavour_18_trigger_time == time: The <span style="color: #b8996f;">**WP**</span>, once an enthusiastic supporter of Brüning's bourgeois government—and the only special interest party to secure a cabinet position—is now scrambling to extricate itself from government following its electoral defeat. While the party reports gains from expanding its electoral base into new states, these are more than offset by disproportionately heavy losses in the regions where it first found success. The same trend can also be observed with the <span style="color: #7FCEB1;">**CNBLP**</span>. Currently, a divisive internal struggle has emerged between chairman Hermann Drewitz and the party’s anti-Brüning faction on the right. The latter seeks to compel the resignation of their sole minister from the government, aiming to pressure Brüning into abandoning his reliance on our votes and instead forming a coalition with the <span style="color: #7A3C00;">**NSDAP**</span>. ?]{!</p>!}
{!<p class="dnvp">!}[? if flavour_29_trigger and flavour_29_trigger_time == time: The <span style="color: #b8996f;">**WP**</span>, once an enthusiastic supporter of Brüning's bourgeois government, is now scrambling to distance itself following its electoral defeat. While the party has gained from expanding into new states, these are more than outweighed by steep losses in its original strongholds. A similar trend can be seen with the <span style="color: #7FCEB1;">**CNBLP**</span>. An internal power struggle has emerged between the pro-Brüning faction—led by party chairman Hermann Drewitz and Justice Minister Johann Bredt—and increasingly anti-Brüning district organizations. These anti-Brüning factions are demanding that the party pressure Brüning into forming a coalition with the <span style="color: #7A3C00;">**NSDAP**</span>[? if spd_toleration:&nbsp;to reduce his dependence on our votes?], or they threaten to dissolve or even break away from the party. ?]{!</p>!}
{!<p class="other">!}[? if year = 1930 and month = 10 and spd_prussia: The <span style="color: #3E88B3;">**Stahlhelm**</span> is once again attempting to destabilize and polarize Germany amidst the economic depression following the failed Young Plan referendum. At their annual congress, the <span style="color: #3E88B3;">**Stahlhelm**</span> announced plans for a referendum to prematurely dissolve the Prussian Landtag. Frustrated by the dominant position the <span style="color: #c00000;">socialists</span> have held in Prussia since the founding of the Weimar Republic, they seek to force new elections that would better reflect the national shift to the right. [? if dnvp_ideology == "Radical":Despite strong enthusiasm from other right-wing parties,?][? if dnvp_ideology == "Moderate":This is met with skepticism from the right-wing parties, and?] Hitler himself has shown little interest in the initiative and has refused to commit the <span style="color: #7A3C00;">**NSDAP**</span>, even as some <span style="color: #7A3C00;">Nazi</span> leaders express strong support. [? if dvp_ideology == "Right" and not in_grand_coalition_prussia and (dvp_leader == "Scholz" or dvp_leader == "Dingeldey"): The <span style="color: #C0A054;">**DVP**</span> was also invited to join the "national front," but its leadership has refused to commit as long as the <span style="color: #7A3C00;">Nazis</span> remain undecided. The fact that they haven’t outright rejected the offer, is a testament to how far they have strayed from democracy.?] At this stage, the success of the plan appears to hinge on Hitler’s support. ?]{!</p>!}
{!<p class="other">!}[? if year = 1930 and month = 10 and dvp_leader == "Scholz" and not lvp_formed and not lvp_merger_2_timer : Walther Schnell has taken concrete steps in pursuit of his goal of bourgeois concentration, inviting officials from all over Germany to a special conference. The group believes that a parliamentary group of the bourgeois parties isn't enough, and more drastic steps have to be made for the survival of the German middle. Schnell's group aims to take charge in attempting to convince the <span style="color: #C0A054;">**DVP**</span>'s national leadership to support his goals during this month's meeting of the <span style="color: #C0A054;">**DVP**</span>'s Executive Committee, which has, unfortunately for them, been delayed until next month. ?]{!</p>!}
{!<p class="other">!}[? if year = 1930 and month = 10 and dvp_leader != "Scholz" and not lvp_formed and not lvp_merger_2_timer : The severe atrophy of the <span style="color: #C0A054;">**DVP**</span>'s vote share and party organizations has sparked internal opposition against their party leader [+ dvp_leader +]. Many within the party’s right wing attribute this downturn to its perceived closeness of its leadership with the <span style="color: #c00000;">**SPD**</span>. ?]{!</p>!}
{!<p class="other">!}[? if year = 1930 and month = 10: <span style="color: #009739;">Br</span><span style="color: #FEDD00;">az</span><span style="color: #009739;">il</span> has undergone a revolution, replacing the First Republic with the Second Republic and bringing Getúlio Vargas into power following a disputed presidential election result earlier in the year. ?]{!</p>!}
{!<p class="dnvp">!}[? if year = 1930 and month = 10 and dnvp_ideology == "Moderate" and (dnvp_leader != "Hergt" or spd_prussia): [? if dnvp_leader == "Hergt": The <span style="color: #3E88B3;">**Stahlhelm**</span>'s plan to destabilize German democracy by launching a referendum to dissolve the Prussian Landtag has sparked divisions within the <span style="color: #3E88B3;">**DNVP**</span>. While many in the party support joining the initiative to end <span style="color: #c00000;">socialist</span> control over Prussia, Treviranus and his Young Conservatives oppose it, as it would require cooperation with the <span style="color: #7A3C00;">**NSDAP**</span>. ?][? if dnvp_leader == "Lambach": Lambach and his supporters have introduced a new party program, notably downplaying monarchism in favor of concrete policies aimed at improving the lives of German workers. This shift has outraged the old guard, who see it as proof that Lambach is advancing the vision he outlined in his "Lambach Article" years ago. With the party congress approaching next month, they are now openly plotting to remove him. At least he has secured Treviranus's support. ?][? if dnvp_leader == "Treviranus": Treviranus and his supporters have introduced a proposal for a renewal of the <span style="color: #3E88B3;">**DNVP**</span>'s platform, shifting towards a more <span style="color: #0087DC;">Christian Democratic</span> course. This proposal omits major mentions of monarchism and tacitly supports the current system, albeit with greater presidential powers. This has greatly angered the old guard, [? if not true_christian_democracy: and with their influence over Treviranus, forced him to withdraw the proposal and maintain the status quo.?][? if true_christian_democracy: who believe that monarchism is a core part of the <span style="color: #3E88B3;">**DNVP**</span>.?] These differences will have to be resolved in the party congress next month. ?] ?]{!</p>!}
{!<p class="other">!}[? if flavour_58_trigger and flavour_58_trigger_time == time: The National Rural League (RLB) has undergone a significant leadership change due to the growing radicalization caused by the worsening economic and agricultural crisis. This has led to a revolt against the Brüning cabinet's farm policy. As a result, Schiele, the Agricultural Minister and chairman of the RLB, is forced to resign, along with the <span style="color: #7FCEB1;">**CNBLP**</span>'s Karl Hepp from the RLB's leadership. In their place, Count Eberhard von Kalckreuth, an arch-reactionary and Hugenberg supporter, is elected as the new President. ?]{!</p>!}
{!<p class="dnvp">!}[? if flavour_57_trigger and flavour_57_trigger_time == time: The Brüning cabinet has introduced the Eastern Aid Act, a policy aimed at relieving the debts of agricultural enterprises in the east—despite the significant financial burden it imposes. This measure directly contradicts Brüning’s broader strategy of economic recovery through budget austerity. To partially fund the policy, the government has retained outdated reparation taxes that became obsolete after the Young Plan. However, the lack of full funding for debt relief has frustrated many influential landowners, prompting them to take their complaints directly to Hindenburg, over whom they wield considerable influence. ?]{!</p>!}
{!<p class="other">!}[? if year = 1930 and month = 11 and dvp_leader == "Scholz" and not lvp_formed and not lvp_merger_2_timer : Schnell's group has successfully elected Eduard Dingeldey to the position of deputy-chairman to Scholz himself. The selection was justified by Scholz's dual role as both chairman of the <span style="color: #C0A054;">**DVP**</span> and head of the Reichstag delegation, and his ongoing health problems. This move was designed to shift the responsibility for revitalizing the <span style="color: #C0A054;">**DVP**</span> and implementing Schnell’s plan for a united bourgeois party away from Scholz and toward a more cooperative figure. Due to Scholz's ill health following the appointment of Dingeldey, has ultimately chosen to resign in favor of him. ?]{!</p>!}
{!<p class="other">!}[? if year = 1930 and month = 11 and dvp_leader != "Scholz" and not lvp_formed and not lvp_merger_2_timer : [? if dvp_leader == "Curtius": The <span style="color: #C0A054;">**DVP**</span>'s right wing has successfully established a new deputy chairman position, appointing Eduard Dingeldey with the goal of shifting some of Curtius's responsibilities to him [? if foreign_minister_party == "DVP":due to his preoccupation with his duties as Foreign Minister?][? if foreign_minister_party != "DVP":due to his involvement in governmental affairs?]. However, Curtius has objected to this move, citing Dingeldey's conservative stances—particularly on cooperation with the <span style="color: #c00000;">**SPD**</span>—and has refused to delegate party leadership to him. This has prompted the <span style="color: #C0A054;">**DVP**</span>'s right wing to begin plotting his removal in next month's Executive Committee meeting.?][? if dvp_leader == "Luther": The broad support Hans Luther enjoyed when he was first elected as leader of the <span style="color: #C0A054;">**DVP**</span> last year has eroded, largely due to his stance on constructive cooperation with the <span style="color: #c00000;">**SPD**</span>. This position has significantly dampened the enthusiasm of the business community, which initially welcomed his leadership. Now, the <span style="color: #C0A054;">**DVP**</span>'s right wing is plotting his removal in next month's Executive Committee meeting.?] ?]{!</p>!}
{!<p class="other">!}[? if year = 1930 and month = 11 and dstp_collapsed and not lvp_formed and not lvp_merger_2_timer : The <span style="color: #D3C24D;">**[+ ddp_name +]**</span> has formally held their party congress to finalize the process of forming the <span style="color: #D3C24D;">**State Party**</span>. In an effort for the <span style="color: #D3C24D;">**[+ ddp_name +]**</span> to be different from its predecessor, Hermann Dietrich has taken an effort to exclude the pacifists and radical democrats from the new party, to create a more conservative appearance. This has caused the remaining far-left <span style="color: #D3C24D;">**DDP**</span> members to split away, forming the insignificant <span style="color: #E0B0FF;">Radical Democratic Party</span> (<span style="color: #E0B0FF;">**RDP**</span>), only joined by a few Young Democrats. Sadly, good intentions and noble ideas were insufficient in themselves to build a political party. ?]{!</p>!}
{!<p class="other">!}[? if year = 1930 and month = 11 and dstp_maintained and not lvp_formed and not lvp_merger_2_timer : The <span style="color: #D3C24D;">**[+ ddp_name +]**</span> has officially held its party congress to finalize the formation of the <span style="color: #D3C24D;">**State Party**</span>. The increased number of <span style="color: #D3D3A9;">**VNR**</span> delegates has led the party to adopt a more nationalist platform, using terms like 'national,' 'social,' and '*Volksgemeinschaft*,' while omitting references to '<span style="color: #586593;">democracy</span>' or '<span style="color: #FFCC00;">liberalism</span>.' Additionally, the party has worked to exclude pacifists and radical democrats in order to project a more conservative image. This has caused the remaining far-left <span style="color: #D3C24D;">**DDP**</span> members to split away, forming the insignificant <span style="color: #E0B0FF;">Radical Democratic Party</span> (<span style="color: #E0B0FF;">**RDP**</span>), only joined by a few Young Democrats. Sadly, good intentions and noble ideas were insufficient in themselves to build a political party. ?]{!</p>!}
{!<p class="other">!}[? if year = 1930 and month = 11 : The <span style="color: #C8102E;">Austrian Social Democrats</span> have won the recent legislative election, securing a majority of seats in their <i>Nationalrat</i>. However a right-wing coalition of the <span style="color: #000000;">Christian Social Party</span> and minor bourgeois parties once again has excluded them from government. The Austrian branch of the <span style="color: #7A3C00;">**NSDAP**</span> have received 3% of the vote, and the political wing of the <span style="color: #ABDDA4;">Heimwehr</span> (comparable to our <i>Freikorps</i>) garnered 6%. This is particularly concerning, as the <span style="color: #C8102E;">Austrian Social Democrats</span> lost votes despite the economic depression occurring while they were in opposition, suggesting the electorate is looking for answers on the right. ?]{!</p>!}
{!<p class="dnvp">!}[? if flavour_15_trigger and flavour_15_trigger_time == time: The combination of our influence over the Brüning government, the ineffectiveness of his agrarian relief plan, the radicalization of the rural electorate signaled by Martin Schiele's resignation from the RLB, and the <span style="color: #7FCEB1;">**CNBLP**</span>'s third-place finish in rural areas behind the <span style="color: #3E88B3;">**DNVP**</span> and <span style="color: #7A3C00;">**NSDAP**</span> has led the party to reconsider its support for Brüning. An anti-Brüning right-wing faction now dominates within the <span style="color: #7FCEB1;">**CNBLP**</span>, successfully pushing through a resolution chastising the Chancellor and demanding a reorganization of the coalition to include the <span style="color: #7A3C00;">Nazis</span>. Brüning has refused to comply, prompting the <span style="color: #7FCEB1;">**CNBLP**</span> to become one of the first parties to bolt from his coalition, now voting against his decrees and supporting right-wing motions of no confidence. ?]{!</p>!}
{!<p class="dnvp">!}[? if flavour_25_trigger and flavour_25_trigger_time == time: The general ineffectiveness of Brüning's agrarian relief, led by Agriculture Minister Martin Schiele of the [? if dnvp_split_2: <span style="color: #7FCEB1;">**CNBLP**</span>?][? if kvp_formed: <span style="color: #0087DC;">**KVP**</span>?][? if dnvp_ideology == "Moderate": <span style="color: #3E88B3;">**DNVP**</span>?], has led to growing dissatisfaction within his own party. To retain their support, Schiele and Brüning have introduced a formal <i>Eastern Aid Act</i>, a policy designed to relieve the debts of agricultural enterprises in the east—despite the heavy financial burden it imposes. While this measure has placated organized agriculture and Hindenburg himself, it stands in direct contradiction to Brüning’s broader strategy of economic recovery through a balanced budget. ?]{!</p>!}
{!<p class="other">!}[? if year = 1930 and month = 12 and kpd_leader == "Thälmann" : Ernst Thälmann has embraced a new <span style='color: #7A3C00;'>national-populist</span> strategy, rallying under the slogan of the <i>Volksrevolution</i>. This move is an attempt by the <span style='color: #700000;'>**KPD**</span> to expand its influence in the bourgeoisie social groups where the <span style='color: #7A3C00;'>**NSDAP**</span> are the strongest in. Leon Trotsky and many other <span style='color: #700000;'>**Communists**</span> have condemned this shift, denouncing the use of 'people's revolution' instead of 'proletarian revolution' as a distortion of <span style="color: #9B0000;">Marxism</span>. They also pointed out that its the exact same slogan used by Otto Strasser and the left-wing faction of the <span style='color: #7A3C00;'>**NSDAP**</span>.  ?]{!</p>!}
{!<p class="dnvp">!}[? if flavour_17_trigger and flavour_17_trigger_time == time: The defection of the northern wing’s Reichstag members from the <span style="color: #007A00;">German Peasants' Party</span> to the <span style="color: #D3C24D;">**[+ ddp_name +]**</span> has effectively ended the party as a national political force. All that remains is the original <span style="color: #38C538;">Bavarian Peasants’ League</span>, which, amid a prevailing mood of resignation, is expected to secure only a fraction of its former support in the next election, as most peasants turn further to the right in search of a solution to Germany's crisis. ?]{!</p>!}
{!<p class="dnvp">!}[? if flavour_19_trigger and flavour_19_trigger_time == time: [? if bruning_time: The <span style="color: #b8996f;">**WP**</span> has issued a vote of no confidence against Brüning, formally ending any possibility of reconciliation with his government and joining the growing ranks of once-supportive bourgeois parties now in opposition. This decision comes after the party’s sole minister resigned under pressure from its anti-Brüning right wing. He is not alone in stepping down, the party’s?][? if not bruning_time: The <span style="color: #b8996f;">**WP**</span>'s?] vice chairman and effective propagandist Otto Colosser has also resigned in protest against Drewitz’s "one-man dictatorship," further accusing him of financial impropriety and other shenanigans. The internal strife has escalated to such an extent that numerous local branches have defected, effectively ending the <span style="color: #b8996f;">**WP**</span> as a viable national political force. ?]{!</p>!}
{!<p class="dnvp">!}[? if flavour_30_trigger and flavour_30_trigger_time == time: Despite pressure from the <span style="color: #b8996f;">**WP**</span>'s anti-Brüning right wing for its lone minister, Johann Bredt, to resign, party chairman Hermann Drewitz has successfully rallied a majority of the parliamentary delegation to support him [? if not in_right_coalition and not in_center_right_coalition: after securing key concessions for the middle class in negotiations with Brüning?][? if in_right_coalition or in_center_right_coalition: after persuading the wider cabinet to abandon tax increases that would have affected the middle class?]. However, this victory comes at a cost—vice chairman and chief propagandist Otto Colosser has resigned in protest, denouncing Drewitz’s 'one-man dictatorship' and accusing him of financial impropriety and other misconduct. The internal strife has escalated to such an extent that numerous local branches have defected, severely weakening the <span style="color: #b8996f;">**WP**</span>'s standing as a viable national political force. ?]{!</p>!}
{!<p class="other">!}[? if year = 1931 and month = 1 and dvp_leader == "Dingeldey" and not bourgeois_list and (dnvp_split_2 or kvp_formed) and not lvp_formed and not lvp_merger_2_timer: Despite previous setbacks for liberal and bourgeois unity, the push for closer ties between centrist and moderate right-wing parties remains alive, particularly at the local level. Politicians from the [? if dstp_formed: <span style="color: #E0B0FF;">**Radical Democrats**</span>?][? if not dstp_formed: <span style="color: #D3C24D;">**Democrats**</span>?], the <span style="color: #0087DC;">**KVP**</span>, and the <span style="color: #8ED8E4;">**CSVD**</span> in the State of Hesse have met to discuss closer cooperation. Their initiative has received a surprisingly positive response, leading to the formation of a new national organization and invitations to figures like Hans Luther and Otto Gessler. However, the effort has been disappointing, as many declined to participate[? if spd_prussia:—largely due to the deep divisions caused by the <span style="color: #3E88B3;">**Stahlhelm**</span>'s planned Prussian referendum, which has split the <span style="color: #D3C24D;">**[+ ddp_name +]**</span> from the rest of the moderate right.?][? if not spd_prussia:—largely due to the strong animosity between the leadership of the national parties.?]?]{!</p>!}
{!<p class="other">!}[? if flavour_23_trigger and flavour_23_trigger_time == time: [+ dvp_leader +] of the <span style="color: #C0A054;">**DVP**</span> has attempted to pressure Brüning into reorganizing his cabinet by removing relatively left-leaning ministers, including their own Julius Curtius, the <span style="color: #D3C24D;">**[+ ddp_name +]**</span>'s Vice-Chancellor Hermann Dietrich, and the [? if z_party_name != "CVP": <span style="color: #000000;">Center</span>?][? if z_party_name == "CVP": <span style="color: #000000;">**CVP**</span>?]'s Interior Minister Joseph Wirth. However, these efforts failed to secure majority support within the <span style="color: #C0A054;">**DVP**</span>'s Reichstag delegation, forcing [+ dvp_leader +] to put the plan on hold, as he still resents how [+ chancellor +] has effectively excluded his supporting parties in the formulation of his decrees. In the meantime, [+ dvp_leader +] has been working on establishing closer relations with the "<span style="color: #7A3C00;">national</span> <span style="color: #3E88B3;">opposition</span>". ?]{!</p>!}
{!<p class="other">!}[? if flavour_39_trigger and flavour_39_trigger_time == time: [? if lvp_leader == "Dingeldey": Dingeldey of the <span style="color: #FFCC00;">**LVP**</span> has been scheming to pressure Brüning into removing relatively left-leaning ministers, including their own Vice-Chancellor Hermann Dietrich and the [? if z_party_name != "CVP": <span style="color: #000000;">Center</span>?][? if z_party_name == "CVP": <span style="color: #000000;">**CVP**</span>?]'s Interior Minister Joseph Wirth. However, these efforts have sparked strong backlash from nearly all corners of the party. Still, an anti-Brüning right wing is slowly emerging, fueled by resentment over how [+ chancellor +] has effectively excluded his supporting parties from the formulation of his decrees. ?][? if lvp_leader != "Dingeldey": The exclusion of political parties from most of the decision-making in Brüning's government has fueled resentment among those who once enthusiastically supported him, including the <span style="color: #FFCC00;">**LVP**</span>. Some members of their parliamentary delegation, particularly those from the right wing of the <span style="color: #C0A054;">**DVP**</span>, many of whom are also members of the <span style="color: #3E88B3;">**Stahlhelm**</span>, have publicly criticized certain left-leaning ministers, including their own Vice-Chancellor Hermann Dietrich and the [? if z_party_name != "CVP": <span style="color: #000000;">Center</span>?][? if z_party_name == "CVP": <span style="color: #000000;">**CVP**</span>?]'s Interior Minister Joseph Wirth. ?] ?]{!</p>!}
{!<p class="other">!}[? if flavour_35_trigger and flavour_35_trigger_time == time: [+ dvp_leader +] of the <span style="color: #C0A054;">**DVP**</span> has attempted to pressure Brüning into reorganizing his cabinet by removing relatively left-leaning ministers, including their own Julius Curtius, the <span style="color: #D3C24D;">**[+ ddp_name +]**</span>'s Vice-Chancellor Hermann Dietrich. However, these efforts failed to secure support within the <span style="color: #C0A054;">**DVP**</span>'s Reichstag delegation, forcing [+ dvp_leader +] to abandon his plans. The inclusion of the right-wing <span style="color: #3E88B3;">**DNVP**</span> in the government has led the <span style="color: #C0A054;">**DVP**</span>’s right wing to abandon its efforts to court Hugenberg’s <span style="color: #003755;">**DNF**</span> and Hitler’s <span style="color: #7A3C00;">**NSDAP**</span>, as they believe the cabinet is already sufficiently right-wing. ?]{!</p>!}
{!<p class="other">!}[? if flavour_56_trigger and flavour_56_trigger_time == time: Dingeldey and the right wing of the <span style="color: #C0A054;">**DVP**</span> attempted to pressure Brüning into reorganizing his cabinet by removing relatively left-leaning ministers, including their own Julius Curtius and the <span style="color: #D3C24D;">**[+ ddp_name +]**</span>'s Vice-Chancellor Hermann Dietrich. However, these efforts failed to gain traction within the <span style="color: #C0A054;">**DVP**</span>'s Reichstag delegation, and [+ dvp_leader +] strongly condemned the move to oust their own ministers. While the party’s right wing will remain a thorn in Brüning’s side, for now, they are little more than noise. ?]{!</p>!}
{!<p class="other">!}[? if flavour_43_trigger and flavour_43_trigger_time == time: Dingeldey of the <span style="color: #FFCC00;">**LVP**</span> has been scheming to pressure Brüning into removing relatively left-leaning ministers, including their own Vice-Chancellor Hermann Dietrich and the [? if z_party_name != "CVP": <span style="color: #000000;">Center</span>?][? if z_party_name == "CVP": <span style="color: #000000;">**CVP**</span>?]'s Interior Minister Joseph Wirth. However, these efforts have sparked strong backlash from nearly all corners of the party, causing him to abandon his plans. ?]{!</p>!}
{!<p class="other">!}[? if year = 1931 and month = 2 and bruning_time and not bruning_event_timer: Hugenberg's [? if dnvp_ideology == "Radical": <span style="color: #3E88B3;">**DNVP**</span>?][? if dnvp_ideology == "Moderate": <span style="color: #003755;">**DNF**</span>?] and the <span style="color: #7A3C00;">**NSDAP**</span> have walked out of the Reichstag in protest against Brüning's government, forming a loose federation known as the <span style="color: #7A3C00;">national</span> [? if dnvp_ideology == "Radical": <span style="color: #3E88B3;">opposition</span>?][? if dnvp_ideology == "Moderate": <span style="color: #003755;">opposition</span>?]. ?]{!</p>!}
{!<p class="other">!}[? if year = 1931 and month = 2 and saxony_nsdap: Our vote of no confidence against Gregor Strasser in the Saxon cabinet has succeeded, with support from the bourgeois parties. The repeated insults and slanders from the <span style="color: #7A3C00;">Nazis</span> toward other cabinet members, along with Strasser’s anti-capitalist views, have caused a breakdown in cooperation with the bourgeois parties. As a result, we have now entered a toleration agreement with them to maintain stability in the government. ?]{!</p>!}
{!<p class="dnvp">!}[? if flavour_16_trigger and flavour_16_trigger_time == time: Four members of the <span style="color: #7FCEB1;">**CNBLP**</span>’s Reichstag delegation have defected, joining the <span style="color: #7A3C00;">Nazis</span> in the national opposition. The internal split between the pro-Brüning moderates and the anti-Brüning radical right has shattered the party’s already fragile unity and damaged its standing among its own electorate. The <span style="color: #7FCEB1;">**CNBLP**</span> has all but ceased to exist as a viable political force. ?]{!</p>!}
{!<p class="other">!}[? if year = 1931 and month = 2 and dstp_formed and not lvp_formed and not lvp_merger_2_timer : Most local organizations that have formerly belonged to the <span style="color: #D3C24D;">**DDP**</span> are extremely hesitant about becoming involved in the new party, and many of the <span style="color: #D3C24D;">**DDP**</span> auxiliary organizations, such as their youth and women's groups have collapsed and need to be built anew. All of this combined with the heavy psychological toll under which the leaders of the <span style="color: #D3C24D;">**State Party**</span> were already laboring as a result of the deepening economic crisis to produce a mood of growing resignation and pessimism about the future of the party.?]{!</p>!}
{!<p class="both">!}[? if year = 1931 and month = 3 and PLR_launched : The <span style="color: #7A3C00;">**NSDAP**</span> has finally agreed to support the referendum for the dissolution of the Landtag[? if not hitler_deported:, though Hitler remains personally skeptical about its chances of success?].[? if dnvp_ideology == "Moderate": With backing from both the <span style="color: #3E88B3;">**Stahlhelm**</span> and the <span style="color: #7A3C00;">**NSDAP**</span>, the <span style="color: #3E88B3;">**DNVP**</span> has also thrown its support behind the initiative. [? if dnvp_leader == "Treviranus": However, Treviranus remains hesitant, but still allows his party members to vote according to their conscience. ?]?] [? if landtag_ref_dvp: Additionally, the <span style="color: #C0A054;">**DVP**</span> has aligned with the <span style="color: #7A3C00;">far</span>-<span style="color: #3E88B3;">right</span>, with Dingeldey reaffirming his party’s commitment to the struggle against <span style="color: #c00000;">socialism</span>.?] Fortunately, the <span style="color: #700000;">**KPD**</span> has refused to cooperate with figures like Hugenberg and [+ nsdap_leader +] in this initiative[? if kpd_leader != "Conciliators":, keeping the anti-democratic vote divided?]. ?]{!</p>!}
{!<p class="other">!}[? if year = 1931 and month = 3 and kpd_leader == "Thälmann" : Richard Scheringer, a former Reichswehr officer and prominent member of the <span style='color: #7A3C00;'>**NSDAP**</span> and <span style='color: #7A3C00;'>**SA**</span>, was imprisoned for attempting to establish <span style='color: #7A3C00;'>Nazi</span> cells within the military. In a shocking turn, after extensive discussions with his <span style='color: #700000;'>**Communist**</span> cellmates, he has defected to the <span style='color: #700000;'>**KPD**</span>. Seizing this opportunity, the <span style="color: #700000;">**KPD**</span> is now embracing the so-called 'Scheringer Line,' incorporating more nationalist rhetoric into its messaging to attract <span style="color: #7A3C00;">Nazi</span> defectors. ?]{!</p>!}
{!<p class="dnvp">!}[? if flavour_27_trigger and flavour_27_trigger_time == time: The Eastern Aid Act, introduced months ago to relieve the debts of agricultural enterprises in the east, has created tension within the coalition, as it directly contradicts Brüning’s goal of maintaining a balanced budget. The reparation tax—now obsolete due to the Young Plan—continues to be used to fund agricultural subsidies, sparking fierce protests from the [? if not lvp_formed: <span style="color: #C0A054;">**DVP**</span>?][? if lvp_formed: <span style="color: #FFCC00;">**LVP**</span>?]. They argue that this tax, primarily levied on businesses, is an unreasonable burden on job creators given the current economic crisis. Fortunately for Brüning, the [? if budget >= 0: budget surplus?][? if reparations == -2: pause in reparations?] has enabled him to abolish the tax while still maintaining full funding for the program. ?]{!</p>!}
{!<p class="dnvp">!}[? if flavour_28_trigger and flavour_28_trigger_time == time: The Eastern Aid Act, introduced months ago to relieve the debts of agricultural enterprises in the east, has created tension within the coalition, as it directly contradicts Brüning’s goal of maintaining a balanced budget. The reparation tax—now obsolete due to the Young Plan—continues to be used to fund agricultural subsidies, sparking fierce protests from the [? if not lvp_formed: <span style="color: #C0A054;">**DVP**</span>?][? if lvp_formed: <span style="color: #FFCC00;">**LVP**</span>?]. They argue that this tax, primarily levied on businesses, is an unreasonable burden on job creators given the current economic crisis. Brüning now finds himself caught between appeasing big industry on one side and placating organized agriculture and Hindenburg on the right. ?]{!</p>!}
{!<p class="dnvp">!}[? if flavour_26_trigger and flavour_26_trigger_time == time: The Brüning cabinet has introduced the Eastern Aid Act, a policy aimed at relieving the debts of agricultural enterprises in the east—despite the significant financial burden it imposes. This measure directly contradicts Brüning’s broader strategy of economic recovery through budget austerity. To partially fund the policy, the government has retained outdated reparation taxes that became obsolete after the Young Plan. However, the lack of full funding for debt relief has frustrated many influential landowners, prompting them to take their complaints directly to Hindenburg, over whom they wield considerable influence. ?]{!</p>!}
{!<p class="other">!}[? if flavour_13_trigger and flavour_13_trigger_time == time: Brüning’s decision to sideline the Reichstag and its parties from any meaningful role in shaping his fiscal and economic policies severely undermined the legitimacy of the bourgeois parties in the eyes of their traditional electorates, due to their powerlessness to dismantle his more repugnant policies. One clear example is the plight of civil servants, who have borne the brunt of salary cuts of approximately 20 percent, pushing many toward the <span style="color: #7A3C00;">Nazis</span>. His deflationary and austerity measures deepened the economic crisis, radicalizing the very social groups upon which these moderate parties relied for electoral support. This has led to heightened tensions between Brüning and his supporting parties, some of whom are even threatening to abandon his coalition. ?]{!</p>!}
{!<p class="other">!}[? if flavour_14_trigger and flavour_14_trigger_time == time: Brüning’s decision to give parliament a significant role in passing and shaping his fiscal and economic policies[? if in_right_coalition or in_center_right_coalition:, rather than ruling through emergency decree, ?] has helped maintain the legitimacy of the bourgeois parties in the eyes of their traditional electorates. The ability of <span style="color: #b8996f;">special</span> <span style="color: #7FCEB1;">interest</span> and <span style="color: #FFCC00;">liberal</span> parties to dismantle some of his most repugnant policies—such as cuts to civil servant salaries—and to pressure him into increasing agrarian relief has reinforced their credibility. However, this has also led to tensions between Brüning and his supporting parties, as he insists that certain sacrifices are necessary to properly implement his economic plan. ?]{!</p>!}
{!<p class="other">!}[? if year = 1931 and month = 4 and PLR_launched : The collection of signatures for the proposed Prussian Landtag referendum has ended, with [+ landtag_ref_signatures +]% voting in favor of the petition. [? if landtag_ref_signatures < 20: Therefore, with a 20% quota, the petition has failed. This is a small victory for the survival of our democracy, and has demoralized and wasted the funds of the <span style="color: #7A3C00;">far</span>-<span style="color: #3E88B3;">right</span>. ?][? if landtag_ref_signatures >= 20: Therefore, with a 20% quota, the petition has passed. This is unexpected, and now we urgently need to step up our propaganda game, to rival the storm from the <span style="color: #3E88B3;">right</span>. The plebiscite is set to be voted on this August.?] ?]{!</p>!}
{!<p class="other">!}[? if year = 1931 and month = 4 and thuringia_nsdap: Our vote of no confidence against Wilhelm Frick of the Thuringian cabinet has succeeded, with the support of the bourgeois parties. The repeated insults and slanders from the <span style="color: #7A3C00;">Nazis</span> toward other members of the Thuringian cabinet have led to the breakdown of bourgeois cooperation with them. As a result, we are now in a toleration agreement with the bourgeois. ?]{!</p>!}
{!<p class="other">!}[? if year == 1931 and month == 4 and dvp_leader == "Dingeldey" and not lvp_formed: In an effort to reverse the <span style="color: #C0A054;">**DVP**</span>’s declining fortunes and stem further defections to the <span style="color: #7A3C00;">far</span>-<span style="color: #3E88B3;">right</span>, the party leadership has adopted a new approach to propaganda, drawing inspiration from the <span style="color: #7A3C00;">Nazis</span>. They have published a program that reframes traditional <span style="color: #FFCC00;">liberal</span> goals through the language of the <span style="color: #7A3C00;">far</span>-<span style="color: #3E88B3;">right</span>. For example, on the issue of individual rights, the program commits the <span style="color: #C0A054;">**DVP**</span> to defending the rights of “the ethically responsible individual”—defined as someone whose individuality is rooted in faith and religiosity—against <span style="color: #700000;">revolution</span>, <span style="color: #c00000;">socialism</span>, and materialism. This is a clear retreat from the unabashed defense of <span style="color: #FFCC00;">liberal</span> principles that had characterized Stresemann’s tenure as party chairman. ?]{!</p>!}
{!<p class="other">!}[? if year = 1931 and month = 4 : The Second Spanish Republic has been established, replacing the monarchy of Alfonso XIII. This follows municipal elections in which republican candidates scored significant victories, winning in all 41 provincial capitals. This has forced the king into exile and a new, young republic has been created. ?]{!</p>!}
{!<p class="other">!}[? if year = 1931 and month = 5 and kpd_leader == "Thälmann" and kpd_relation < 40 : Under orders from the <span style="color: #D50000;">Comintern</span>, the <span style="color: #700000;">**KPD**</span> has abandoned its previous stance of identifying Hitler's <span style="color: #7A3C00;">fascism</span> as the primary enemy. They now claim to be 'correcting' their position by 'reducing the evaluation of <span style="color: #7A3C00;">fascism</span> to its proper proportions', which means reverting back to their <span style="color: #c00000;">social</span> <span style="color: #7A3C00;">fascist</span> stance. To them, we are no different than the actual <span style="color: #7A3C00;">fascists</span>. ?]{!</p>!}
{!<p class="dnvp">!}[? if year = 1931 and month = 6 and dnvp_ideology == "Radical": Hugenberg's attempts to cultivate a Hitler-style personality have failed, as his upper-class elitism and arrogance leave him out of touch with ordinary people suffering from the economic crisis. Frustrated by his lack of charisma, a group of industrialists has even offered a large reward for anyone who can help depose him, convinced he is steering the <span style="color: #3E88B3;">**DNVP**</span> toward disaster. Adding to his troubles, the economic depression has slashed revenues from his media empire, forcing him to cut the <span style="color: #3E88B3;">**DNVP**</span>'s budget by 30% just to keep the party afloat. ?]{!</p>!}
{!<p class="other">!}[? if banking_crisis_timer == 3 : The formation of the Austro-German customs union has prompted <span style="color: #002654;">France</span> to exert significant economic pressure on Austria. Leveraging its sizable influence over the Austrian economy, <span style="color: #002654;">France</span> is not only refusing to extend financial assistance to the country’s struggling major banks but is also deliberately withdrawing large sums of money, further destabilizing Austria's financial sector. ?]{!</p>!}
{!<p class="other">!}[? if banking_crisis_timer == 2 : The major Austrian Bank Creditanstalt has declared bankruptcy, following <span style="color: #002654;">French</span> political and economic pressures aimed at undermining the plans for the Austro-German customs union. In that respect, they have certainly succeeded. We can only hope that this crisis does not spill over into Germany or, worse, spread across Europe. ?]{!</p>!}
{!<p class="other">!}[? if banking_crisis_timer == 1 : Following Creditanstalt's bankruptcy, an event that has shaken international financial markets, investors are now rapidly withdrawing funds from German banks. Germany's heavy reliance on short-term foreign loans has turned Austria’s banking crisis into a direct threat to our own stability. In response, Reichsbank President [? if luther_reichsbank: Hans Luther?][? if not luther_reichsbank: Friedrich Dreyse?] has implemented measures to curb capital flight by disincentivizing the transfer of money abroad. However, the effectiveness of these policies remains uncertain, and may cause unintended consequences. ?]{!</p>!}
{!<p class="other">!}[? if year = 1931 and month == 6 and not kaas_explode_seen and z_leader == "Kaas": Rumors have circulated regarding <span style="color: #000000;">Center Party</span> leader Ludwig Kaas's intention to resign, as he has privately expressed a desire to step down from his burdensome position. His frequent absences from Germany, often spent in Rome, have left his deputy leader Joos and parliamentary leader Brüning to effectively manage the party. Kaas also holds one of the worst attendance records in the Reichstag. However, Kaas has attributed his prolonged absences to poor health, and the considerable autonomy he has granted to his colleagues has quelled efforts to replace him with a more active, though controversial, leader. ?]{!</p>!}
{!<p class="other">!}[? if year = 1931 and month = 6 and dstp_formed and not lvp_formed and not lvp_merger_2_timer : The organizational malaise of the <span style="color: #D3C24D;">**[+ ddp_name +]**</span> is evident in its dwindling membership. Across the country, anywhere from 30% to 60% of those who had belonged to the <span style="color: #D3C24D;">**DDP**</span> in the summer of 1930 had failed to join its successor by the same time this year. In some urban areas, the party’s shift to the right is estimated to have cost it more than half of its former supporters. In states without a strong liberal tradition, the situation is so dire that even the local branches that had once championed the formation of the <span style="color: #D3C24D;">**[+ ddp_name +]**</span> the previous year all but recommended conceding defeat and dissolving the party entirely. ?]{!</p>!}
{!<p class="other">!}[? if year = 1931 and month = 6 : The first elections of the Second Spanish Republic has been held, with it a great victory for the <span style="color: #EF1C27;">Spanish Socialists</span> and the <span style="color: #E25822;">Republican Left</span>. Together, they've formed a government with Manuel Azaña as Prime Minister. ?]{!</p>!}
{!<p class="dnvp">!}[? if flavour_20_trigger and flavour_20_trigger_time == time: The leaders of the <span style="color: #b8996f;">**WP**</span> are struggling desperately to repair the damage caused by the conflict between Drewitz and Colosser, but their efforts are repeatedly undermined by renewed demands for Drewitz’s resignation. In response, Drewitz took his case before the national party congress this month. Backed by strong support from the party’s pro-Brüning faction, he successfully secured re-election as party chairman. Meanwhile, the leaders of several district organizations that had broken away months earlier have returned to the fold just in time for the congress. Perhaps the party can survive after all? ?]{!</p>!}
{!<p class="other">!}[? if flavour_22_trigger and flavour_22_trigger_time == time: [+ dvp_leader +] and the right wing of the <span style="color: #C0A054;">**DVP**</span> are outraged by Brüning's emergency decree, assailing the new tax levies and cuts to civil servant salaries. [+ dvp_leader +] has strongly urged Brüning to restore bourgeois confidence in his government by severing all ties with the <span style="color: #c00000;">**SPD**</span>. To pressure Brüning, [+ dvp_leader +] has threatened to vote to reconvene the Reichstag, to allow the <span style="color: #7A3C00;">national</span> <span style="color: #3E88B3;">opposition</span> to introduce a vote of no confidence with their support. This move has gained significant support among the <span style="color: #C0A054;">**DVP**</span>’s right wing, which has long sought to force a cabinet reshuffle. However, with only a narrow majority of the delegation backing him, Brüning—along with [? if luther_reichsbank: Reichsbank President ?][? if not luther_reichsbank: former chancellor ?] and <span style="color: #C0A054;">**DVP**</span> member Hans Luther—has pleaded for patience, promising a gradual shift to the right and a relaxation of labor laws. Faced with these assurances, as well as threats of resignation from Curtius and other key figures on the party’s left wing, the delegation ultimately reversed its stance. ?]{!</p>!}
{!<p class="other">!}[? if flavour_34_trigger and flavour_34_trigger_time == time: [+ dvp_leader +] and the right wing of the <span style="color: #C0A054;">**DVP**</span> are outraged by Brüning's emergency decree, criticizing the new tax levies and cuts to civil servant salaries. [+ dvp_leader +] has strongly urged Brüning to restore bourgeois confidence in his government by severing all ties with the <span style="color: #c00000;">**SPD**</span>. This push has gained significant support from the <span style="color: #C0A054;">**DVP**</span>’s right wing, which has long advocated for a cabinet reshuffle. In response, Brüning—along with [? if luther_reichsbank: Reichsbank President ?][? if not luther_reichsbank: former chancellor ?] and <span style="color: #C0A054;">**DVP**</span> member Hans Luther—has pleaded for patience, promising a shift to the right in a cabinet reorganization and a relaxation of labor laws. With these assurances, [+ dvp_leader +] is no longer working to bring down the government. ?]{!</p>!}
{!<p class="other">!}[? if flavour_38_trigger and flavour_38_trigger_time == time: [? if lvp_leader == "Dingeldey": Dingeldey and the right wing of the <span style="color: #FFCC00;">**LVP**</span> are disappointed by Brüning's emergency decree, criticizing the new taxes and cuts to civil servant salaries. Dingeldey has personally urged Brüning to form a coalition with the <span style="color: #7A3C00;">national</span> <span style="color: #3E88B3;">opposition</span> to end his reliance on our votes. However, it would be impossible for him to convince the majority of his own party to oppose Brüning, so he is unable to act on his plans—at least for now. ?][? if lvp_leader != "Dingeldey": The <span style="color: #FFCC00;">**LVP**</span> is disappointed by Brüning's emergency decrees, criticizing the new taxes and cuts to civil servant salaries. Some on the party's right wing have urged Brüning to form a coalition with the <span style="color: #7A3C00;">national</span> <span style="color: #3E88B3;">opposition</span> to end his reliance on our votes. However, [+ lvp_leader +] and the wider party remains broadly supportive of his government and will continue working to steer him away from policies that harm the middle class. ?] ?]{!</p>!}
{!<p class="other">!}[? if flavour_42_trigger and flavour_42_trigger_time == time: Dingeldey and the right wing of the <span style="color: #FFCC00;">**LVP**</span> are disappointed by Brüning's emergency decree, criticizing the new taxes and cuts to civil servant salaries. Dingeldey has personally urged Brüning to form a coalition with the <span style="color: #7A3C00;">national</span> <span style="color: #003755;">opposition</span>[? if spd_toleration_right:&nbsp;to end his reliance on our votes?]. However, he is largely satisfied with his government, due to the inclusion of the <span style="color: #3E88B3;">**DNVP**</span>. ?]{!</p>!}
{!<p class="other">!}[? if flavour_45_trigger and flavour_45_trigger_time == time: The <span style="color: #FFCC00;">**LVP**</span> is disappointed by Brüning's emergency decrees thus far, criticizing the new taxes and cuts to civil servant salaries. There is a growing call within the right of the party for Brüning to form a coalition with the <span style="color: #7A3C00;">national</span> <span style="color: #003755;">opposition</span>[? if spd_toleration_right:&nbsp;to end his reliance on our votes?]. However, the party is still largely satisfied with his government, especially with the inclusion of the <span style="color: #3E88B3;">**DNVP**</span>[? if not spd_toleration_right:, effectively locking us into opposition?]. ?]{!</p>!}
{!<p class="dnvp">!}[? if flavour_51_trigger and flavour_51_trigger_time == time: Kurt von Schleicher, who initially convinced Hindenburg to appoint Brüning, is now skeptical—especially in the aftermath of the Banking Crisis—that Brüning can carry out the far-reaching reforms needed to restore Germany’s great power status. As a result, Schleicher is now seeking to establish connections with the anti-parliamentary right. ?]{!</p>!}
{!<p class="other">!}[? if flavour_24_trigger and flavour_24_trigger_time == time: The Banking Crisis[? if emergency_decree_seen:&nbsp;and the fallout from Brüning's second emergency decree?] has significantly intensified anti-Brüning sentiment among the bourgeois parties, especially the <span style="color: #C0A054;">**DVP**</span>[? if emergency_decree_seen:, which only narrowly secured a majority within its own ranks to support Brüning?]. The right wing of the <span style="color: #C0A054;">**DVP**</span> is increasingly frustrated by Brüning’s failure to follow through on his promise to shift the cabinet further to the right. However, [+ dvp_leader +] remains hesitant to bring down the government, fearing it would further destabilize Germany’s financial situation. Additionally, the <span style="color: #7A3C00;">national</span> <span style="color: #3E88B3;">opposition</span> has shown no interest in joining Brüning’s cabinet. Under these circumstances, the <span style="color: #C0A054;">**DVP**</span> has little choice but to continue supporting Brüning. ?]{!</p>!}
{!<p class="other">!}[? if flavour_57_trigger and flavour_57_trigger_time == time: The Banking Crisis[? if emergency_decree_seen: and the fallout from Brüning's second emergency decree?] has significantly intensified anti-Brüning sentiment among the bourgeois parties, especially the <span style="color: #C0A054;">**DVP**</span>[? if emergency_decree_seen:, which only narrowly secured a majority within its own ranks to support Brüning?]. The party’s right wing is growing increasingly frustrated with Brüning’s failure to shift the cabinet further to the right as promised. However, [+ dvp_leader +] argues that such a move would only further destabilize Germany’s fragile financial situation and insists that the <span style="color: #7A3C00;">national</span> <span style="color: #3E88B3;">opposition</span> would never agree to enter government. Despite its grievances, the <span style="color: #C0A054;">**DVP**</span> has little choice but to continue supporting Brüning—for now. ?]{!</p>!}
{!<p class="other">!}[? if flavour_40_trigger and flavour_40_trigger_time == time: [? if lvp_leader == "Dingeldey": The Banking Crisis and the austerity measures that followed has worsened the <span style="color: #FFCC00;">**LVP**</span>'s perception of Brüning's government, and has caused their own leader [+ lvp_leader +] to make public statements criticizing him, despite their own Julius Curtius having been the architect of the disastrous Austro-German customs union in the first place. A significant portion of the <span style="color: #FFCC00;">**LVP**</span> is still opposed to an outright break with the government, especially those who came from the <span style="color: #D3C24D;">**[+ ddp_name +]**</span>. ?][? if lvp_leader != "Dingeldey": The Banking Crisis and the austerity measures that followed has worsened the <span style="color: #FFCC00;">**LVP**</span>'s perception of Brüning's government, despite their own Julius Curtius having been the architect of the disastrous Austro-German customs union in the first place. A small minority of radical-right industrialists and democracy-skeptics within the <span style="color: #FFCC00;">**LVP**</span> advocate for an outright break with the government—they amount to little more than noise, at least for now. ?] ?]{!</p>!}
{!<p class="other">!}[? if flavour_44_trigger and flavour_44_trigger_time == time: The Banking Crisis and the subsequent austerity measures have further soured the <span style="color: #FFCC00;">**LVP**</span>’s perception of Brüning’s government, prompting their leader, Dingeldey, to publicly criticize him—despite the fact that their own Julius Curtius was the architect of the disastrous Austro-German customs union. While a small minority of radical-right industrialists and democracy-skeptics within the <span style="color: #FFCC00;">**LVP**</span> advocate for an outright break with the government, they amount to little more than noise. ?]{!</p>!}
{!<p class="other">!}[? if flavour_46_trigger and flavour_46_trigger_time == time: The Banking Crisis and the subsequent austerity measures have further soured the <span style="color: #FFCC00;">**LVP**</span>’s perception of Brüning’s government, but [+ lvp_leader +] remains a firm supporter of Brüning[? if lvp_leader != "Heuss" and lvp_leader != "Luther":, serving as [? if lvp_leader != "Dietrich": a minister in Brüning's government himself?][? if lvp_leader == "Dietrich": the Vice-Chancellor to Brüning himself?]?]. While a small minority of radical-right industrialists and democracy-skeptics within the <span style="color: #FFCC00;">LVP</span> advocate for an outright break with the government, they amount to little more than noise. ?]{!</p>!}
{!<p class="other">!}[? if flavour_36_trigger and flavour_36_trigger_time == time: The Banking Crisis[? if emergency_decree_seen:&nbsp;and the fallout from Brüning's second emergency decree?][? if emergency_act_seen:&nbsp;and the fallout from Brüning's emergency act?] has intensified anti-Brüning sentiment among the bourgeois parties, especially the <span style="color: #C0A054;">**DVP**</span>[? if emergency_decree_seen:, which already been frustrated with his actions?][? if emergency_act_seen:, which already been frustrated by the lack of consultation with his supporting parties?]. However, [+ dvp_leader +] remains hesitant to bring down the government, fearing it would further destabilize Germany’s financial situation. Additionally, the <span style="color: #7A3C00;">national</span> <span style="color: #003755;">opposition</span> still shows no interest in joining Brüning’s cabinet. Under these circumstances, the <span style="color: #C0A054;">**DVP**</span> has little choice but to continue supporting Brüning. ?]{!</p>!}
{!<p class="other">!}[? if year = 1931 and month == 7 and landtag_ref_signatures_passed and not landtag_ref_dvp and not lvp_formed: The <span style="color: #C0A054;">**DVP**</span> has unexpectedly taken a stand against the Prussian Landtag referendum, campaigning in favor of stability and democracy. This move has disrupted the <span style="color: #3E88B3;">**Stahlhelm**</span>'s plan for a united right-wing front in support of the referendum, significantly reducing its chances of success. ?]{!</p>!}
{!<p class="other">!}[? if year = 1931 and month == 7 and landtag_ref_votes and landtag_ref_dvp and not lvp_formed: The remaining influential members of the <span style="color: #C0A054;">**DVP**</span>'s left wing, has denounced the party’s strong endorsement of the Prussian Landtag referendum. Some even call refusing to support the referendum a "patriotic duty", with Curtius giving a speech over the radio announcing that he won't be following his party's position on the plebiscite. Unfortunately, the pro-democratic forces within the party are powerless and impotent, but every little bit helps. ?]{!</p>!}
{!<p class="other">!}[? if year = 1931 and month == 8 and blutmai: The Berlin <span style="color: #700000;">**KPD**</span> has adopted a policy of retaliatory assassinations: one Berlin police officer for every <span style="color: #700000;">**KPD**</span> member killed by the police. In line with this policy, police captains Paul Anlauf and Franz Lenck have been assassinated by Erich Mielke and Erich Ziemer. After failing to eliminate a third officer who could have identified them, the two gunmen took no chances and fled immediately to Moscow. The killings have provoked widespread outrage against the <span style="color: #700000;">Communists</span> and renewed calls among the bourgeoisie to suppress their movement with urgency. ?]{!</p>!}
{!<p class="dnvp">!}[? if flavour_21_trigger and flavour_21_trigger_time == time: The controversy surrounding <span style="color: #b8996f;">**WP**</span> leader Hermann Drewitz’s scandals has finally forced him to relinquish the party chairmanship to prevent an even more damaging secession than the one that occurred months earlier. [? if not lvp_formed: Many of their Reichstag members, including Otto Colosser, have defected to the <span style="color: #D3C24D;">**[+ ddp_name +]**</span>, which is seeking to capitalize on their internal discord. ?]With ongoing internal strife and resignations of its most effective leaders, the <span style="color: #b8996f;">**WP**</span> is barely able to function as a political force anymore. ?]{!</p>!}
{!<p class="dnvp">!}[? if flavour_31_trigger and flavour_31_trigger_time == time: The controversy surrounding <span style="color: #b8996f;">**WP**</span> leader Hermann Drewitz and his scandals has finally settled, as he steps down from the chairmanship in favor of the highly respected Johann Bredt. This move has drawn Otto Colosser and the rebellious district organizations back into the fold. Despite enduring internal strife and multiple splits, the <span style="color: #b8996f;">**WP**</span> has managed to survive as a national force, continuing to represent the conservative middle class that might have otherwise turned to the <span style="color: #7A3C00;">**NSDAP**</span>. ?]{!</p>!}
{!<p class="dnvp">!}[? if flavour_37_trigger and flavour_37_trigger_time == time: The <span style="color: #3E88B3;">**DNVP**</span> has come under heavy fire from the <span style="color: #7A3C00;">national</span> <span style="color: #003755;">opposition</span> for their continued support of the Brüning cabinet following the Banking Crisis, with the <span style="color: #003755;">**DNF**</span> seeking to win over disillusioned <span style="color: #3E88B3;">nationalists</span>. This has caused great concern within the party, as they're[? if dnvp_votes >= nsdap_votes:&nbsp;threatening to be?] eclipsed by the more radical <span style="color: #7A3C00;">**NSDAP**</span>, whose rise has led the <span style="color: #3E88B3;">**DNVP**</span>’s industrial backers to pressure the party into cooperating with them by forcing Brüning to allow the <span style="color: #7A3C00;">far</span>-<span style="color: #003755;">right</span> into government. ?]{!</p>!}
{!<p class="dnvp">!}[? if flavour_52_trigger and flavour_52_trigger_time == time: The rapid rise of the <span style="color: #7A3C00;">**NSDAP**</span> at the expense of the <span style="color: #3E88B3;">**DNVP**</span> has triggered concern among many members of the party. They blame their association with the unpopular Brüning government for their decline and believe that developing closer ties with the <span style="color: #7A3C00;">national</span> <span style="color: #003755;">opposition</span> is crucial for regaining lost support. As a result, numerous <span style="color: #3E88B3;">**DNVP**</span> members have begun openly criticizing Chancellor Brüning and are actively pushing for a coalition with the national opposition. This doesn't bode well for the future stability of his cabinet. ?]{!</p>!}
{!<p class="other">!}[? if year = 1931 and month == 9 and banking_crisis_seen: The Austro-German customs union is officially dead, with international courts striking down the project. [? if foreign_minister_party == "DVP": Julius Curtius of the <span style="color: #C0A054;">**DVP**</span>, the architect of this project, has suffered a severe blow to his reputation, with growing calls from within his own party for his resignation. [? if bruning_toleration_time and dvp_ideology == "Right": If he steps down, the <span style="color: #C0A054;">**DVP**</span> will no longer have any representation in the cabinet, freeing them to support votes of no confidence against Brüning.?]?]?]{!</p>!}
{!<p class="other">!}[? if year = 1931 and month = 9 : Great Britain has abandoned the Gold Standard! The short-lived naval mutiny, caused by pay cuts, led to panic on the London Stock Exchange and a run on the Pound. The British government then adopted this policy as a means to stimulate industrial exports without the harmful side effects of deflation, achieved through the devaluation of the Pound. Germany's top economic experts viewed this as an act of desperation that would ruin England’s banks and insurance companies. Germany's citizens' fear of inflation, along with the obligation to preserve the mark’s value at 0.358 grams of fine gold under the Young Plan, has prevented Reichsbank President [? if luther_reichsbank: Luther?][? if not luther_reichsbank: Dreyse?] from abandoning the Gold Standard. However, with both the United States and Britain showing sympathies toward our request for more flexible regulations, perhaps this decision was ultimately made on economic grounds after all. ?]{!</p>!}
{!<p class="other">!}[? if year = 1931 and month = 9: The Japanese have invaded Manchuria following the Mukden incident. ?]{!</p>!}
{!<p class="other">!}[? if flavour_41_trigger and flavour_41_trigger_time == time: Following the failure of the Austro-German customs union project and its disastrous consequences, the architect of the plan, Julius Curtius of the [? if not lvp_formed and dvp_exist:<span style="color: #C0A054;">**DVP**</span>?][? if not lvp_formed and not dvp_exist:<span style="color: #D3C24D;">**[+ ddp_name +]**</span>?][? if lvp_formed:<span style="color: #FFCC00;">**LVP**</span>?] has taken responsibility and resigned. This has severely weakened the stability of the Brüning government, as Curtius was one of his strongest supporters. Now, with Brüning taking over the Foreign Ministry, the [? if not lvp_formed:<span style="color: #C0A054;">**DVP**</span>?][? if not lvp_formed and not dvp_exist:<span style="color: #D3C24D;">**[+ ddp_name +]**</span>?][? if lvp_formed:<span style="color: #FFCC00;">**LVP**</span>?] now has less reasons to support his government. [? if dvp_leader == "Curtius" or lvp_leader == "Curtius" and lvp_formed: Furthermore, Curtius has resigned from his party's leadership, and right-wing forces are expected to succeed him as the leader of the [? if not lvp_formed:<span style="color: #C0A054;">**DVP**</span>?][? if lvp_formed:<span style="color: #FFCC00;">**LVP**</span>?], as the pro-Brüning forces are weakened within the party as well.?] ?]{!</p>!}
{!<p class="other">!}[? if year == 1931 and month == 10 and not bourgeois_list and not bourgeois_parliament and (dnvp_split_2 or kvp_formed) and dvp_leader == "Dingeldey" and not lvp_formed and not lvp_merger_2_timer: The <span style="color: #D3C24D;">**[+ ddp_name +]**</span> has reached out to moderate right-wing parties instead of the <span style="color: #C0A054;">**DVP**</span>, whose leader, Dingeldey, doesn't plan to take meaningful action on the issue. This effort stems from a shared fear of being politically destroyed by the <span style="color: #7A3C00;">far</span>-<span style="color: #3E88B3;">right</span>[? if bruning_toleration_time:, and to influence Brüning's legislative agenda?]. Hans Schlange-Schöningen, one of [? if not bruning_toleration_time: the strongest supporters for bourgeois unity?][? if bruning_toleration_time: Brüning's strongest supporters?] in the [? if not kvp_formed: <span style="color: #7FCEB1;">**CNBLP**</span>?][? if kvp_formed: <span style="color: #0087DC;">**KVP**</span>?][? if cnblp_defect:, which has since split from Brüning,?], has pleaded with Westarp[? if csvd_formed:, the <span style="color: #8ED8E4;">**CSVD**</span>,?] and some representatives from the <span style="color: #C0A054;">**DVP**</span> to put aside their differences. [? if bruning_toleration_time: With Brüning's strong encouragement, they agreed to issue a joint statement in the Reichstag affirming their support for his government. However, leaks about the plan surfaced, forcing them to abandon it after multiple parties withdrew from negotiations.?]?]{!</p>!}
{!<p class="other">!}[? if year = 1931 and month = 10: The recent United Kingdom general elections have resulted in the greatest landslide in the country's history, with <span style="color: #f0456a;">Labour</span> receiving only a meager 52 seats, losing 235 of them from the previous election. Despite this, the incumbent <span style="color: #008000;">National Labour</span> Prime Minister Ramsay MacDonald has remained in office, ruling with the <span style="color: #0087DC;">Tories</span>. ?]{!</p>!}
{!<p class="other">!}[? if flavour_47_trigger and flavour_47_trigger_time == time: [? if year == 1931 and month >= 11: During the Hessian Landtag election campaign?][? if year != 1931 or month < 11: While campaigning for local elections?], Dingeldey proposed to Hitler that their parties refrain from attacking each other to present a united front against the <span style="color: #000000;">We</span><span style="color: #DD0000;">im</span><span style="color: #FFCC00;">ar</span> Coalition parties. Their <span style="color: #7A3C00;">far</span>-<span style="color: #3E88B3;">right</span> shift, [? if dvp_defect: coupled with their exit from the Brüning cabinet,?] has galvanized the party’s left wing, now rallying around the "Kunz-Dohna" group, named after its leaders. Their goal is to steer the <span style="color: #C0A054;">**DVP**</span> back to the political course of its late founder and chairman, Stresemann, and away from any alignment with the <span style="color: #7A3C00;">national</span> <span style="color: #3E88B3;">opposition</span>. ?]{!</p>!}
{!<p class="other">!}[? if year = 1931 and month = 12 and (reparations == -2 or hoover_moratorium_seen) and bruning_time: Brüning has increasingly been taken more of an active role in foreign affairs, and have successfully convinced the Western Allies to review Germany's reparations burden once again, citing that the government's attempts to prevent a complete collapse of the economy has left little room to pay reparations, even after a moratorium on debt. The "Beneduce Report" has been produced by a team of professional bankers, concluding not only that Germany could afford no reparations in the foreseeable future, but active efforts to reduce its debt burden should take place. Through a diplomatic offensive, and has resulted in an agreement that dramatically expanded Germany's maneuvering room. ?]{!</p>!}
{!<p class="other">!}[? if flavour_59_trigger and flavour_59_trigger_time == time: Chancellor [+ chancellor +] has publicly distanced himself from efforts to include the <span style="color: #7A3C00;">**NSDAP**</span> in government, questioning their commitment to the path of legality. This comes after Brüning sought to bypass Hugenberg and negotiate directly with Hitler in what became known as the Brüning-Hitler alliance. Meetings between Brüning and the <span style="color: #7A3C00;">**NSDAP**</span>’s Hitler and Strasser suggested that both sides were genuinely interested in reaching an accommodation, with Brüning even redirecting his criticisms away from Hitler and toward Hugenberg. Fortunately, these talks collapsed, as <span style="color: #7A3C00;">Nazi</span> electoral gains convinced them that the path to power did not lie through Brüning. ?]{!</p>!}
{!<p class="other">!}[? if flavour_48_trigger and flavour_48_trigger_time == time: Dingeldey’s reluctance to challenge the <span style="color: #C0A054;">DVP</span>’s industrial wing has strained relations with the DHV, a white-collar union that has consistently supported the party since the republic's founding. His refusal to recognize unions' role in collective bargaining has left the DHV feeling sidelined and questioning its future with the party. The Kunz-Dohna group has decided to raise their concerns at this month's executive committee meeting, where their isolation and impotence is shown when only 14 out of 344 votes are cast against a resolution unequivocally denouncing Brüning. The group is now considering a secession, following those on the extreme right advocating for a merger with [? if dnvp_ideology == "Moderate":the?][? if dnvp_ideology == "Radical":Hugenberg's?] <span style="color: #3E88B3;">**DNVP**</span> in the same meeting. ?]{!</p>!}
{!<p class="other">!}[? if year = 1931 and month = 12 : The new Second Spanish Republic has adopted its new democratic constitution, establishing a secular, progressive, and republican government. Times are looking bright for Spain. ?]{!</p>!}
{!<p class="other">!}[? if flavour_49_trigger and flavour_49_trigger_time == time: Ever since the party executive meeting and the victory of the extreme right-wing forces, Dingeldey has been increasingly under pressure to pursue closer ties to the parties of the <span style="color: #7A3C00;">national</span> <span style="color: #3E88B3;">opposition</span>. Erich von Gilsa, the party's liaison to the <span style="color: #3E88B3;">**Stahlhelm**</span>, has forced Dingeldey to meet with local district organization leaders to pressure the party into closer ties with [? if dnvp_ideology == "Moderate":the?][? if dnvp_ideology == "Radical":Hugenberg's?] <span style="color: #3E88B3;">**DNVP**</span>, and ultimately a merger. [? if dnvp_ideology == "Radical": So far, he has resisted these attempts, arguing that Hugenberg will demand nothing less than a complete absorption of the <span style="color: #C0A054;">**DVP**</span>.?][? if dnvp_ideology == "Moderate": With the decline in the vote share of the bourgeois parties, this option sounds quite appealing, and negotiations between the two parties have begun.?] ?]{!</p>!}
# make version for if dvp never split cuz of many reasons
{!<p class="other">!}[? if flavour_50_trigger and flavour_50_trigger_time == time: Erich von Gilsa has resigned from the <span style="color: #C0A054;">**DVP**</span> as a way of protesting Dingeldey’s lack of interest in a merger with the <span style="color: #3E88B3;">**DNVP**</span>. This has set off a chain of events, with many local district organizations and key leaders on the extreme-right wing of the party resigning and defecting to the <span style="color: #3E88B3;">**DNVP**</span>. [? if bruning_time: Furthermore, when the Reichstag was back in session, Dingeldey attempted to impose party discipline, forcing the delegation to unanimously vote against Brüning, resulting in the expulsion of Curtius and Siegfried von Kardorff, the left wing's most respected spokesmen. ?] It's safe to say that the <span style="color: #C0A054;">**DVP**</span> is no longer a viable national political force. ?]{!</p>!}
{!<p class="dnvp">!}[? if year = 1932 and month = 3 and dnvp_ideology == "Radical" and presidential_election_seen: Recent events in the presidential election—and the betrayal of Hugenberg’s closest allies in favor of the <span style="color: #7A3C00;">**NSDAP**</span>—have left him deeply demoralized, bordering on resignation. Within his own party, calls for new leadership grow louder, as many hope to salvage its fading fortunes. But Hugenberg is Hugenberg. We’ve not seen the last of him. ?]{!</p>!}
{!<p class="other">!}[? if flavour_53_trigger and flavour_53_trigger_time == time: [? if dnvp_leader == "Lambach": The negotiations between the <span style="color: #C0A054;">**DVP**</span> and <span style="color: #3E88B3;">**DNVP**</span> have so far failed, as Lambach's <span style="color: #6D5298;">Christian Social</span> and illiberal ideals clash with the <span style="color: #C0A054;">**DVP**</span> on nearly every issue. However, the radical right faction within the <span style="color: #C0A054;">**DVP**</span> continues to push for closer ties, believing that a merger could force a leadership change within the <span style="color: #3E88B3;">**DNVP**</span>. ?][? if dnvp_leader != "Lambach": The negotiations between the <span style="color: #C0A054;">**DVP**</span> and <span style="color: #3E88B3;">**DNVP**</span> have yielded progress, leading to further cooperation between the two parties. Recognizing their declining vote shares and ideological similarities, they have agreed to run on a joint list in the next election. [? if dnvp_leader == "Treviranus": Treviranus is particularly in favor of closer bourgeois cooperation, as he has long sought the consolidation of the democratic bourgeois right. ?][? if dnvp_leader == "Schiele": Schiele's agrarian conservatism has clashed with the <span style="color: #C0A054;">**DVP**</span>'s industrial capitalism, causing unease among the party's backers, who fear their influence may be diluted within a broader party. ?][? if dnvp_leader == "Hergt" or dnvp_leader == "Westarp": [+ dnvp_leader +] has expressed skepticism about a proposed merger, arguing that despite the <span style="color: #C0A054;">**DVP**</span>'s shift to the right, fundamental differences remain—particularly on issues like monarchism and [? if reparations >= 0: reparations?][? if reparations == -2: the "national questions"?]. ?]?] ?]{!</p>!}
{!<p class="other">!}[? if year = 1932 and month = 4 and pro_republic < 50 and dvp_exist and (luther_reichsbank or dvp_leader == "Luther" or (lvp_leader == "Luther" and lvp_formed)): Hans Luther has been shot! Luckily, he was only injured in the shoulder, and will be able to continue his job as the [? if luther_reichsbank: President of the Reichsbank?][? if dvp_leader == "Luther" or (lvp_leader == "Luther" and lvp_formed): leader of the [? if not lvp_formed:<span style="color: #C0A054;">**DVP**</span>?][? if lvp_formed:<span style="color: #FFCC00;">**LVP**</span>?]?]. [? if luther_reichsbank: The assailants have written a letter beforehand, criticizing his monetary policy?][? if dvp_leader == "Luther" or (lvp_leader == "Luther" and lvp_formed): The assailants have written a letter beforehand, criticizing his leadership over the [? if not lvp_formed:<span style="color: #C0A054;">**DVP**</span>?][? if lvp_formed:<span style="color: #FFCC00;">**LVP**</span>?]?]. ?]{!</p>!}
{!<p class="other">!}[? if year = 1932 and month = 5 and not lvp_formed and not bourgeois_parliament and not bourgeois_list and liberal_vote < 5 and dvp_exist: The destruction of the bourgeois and liberal parties in the face of the <span style="color: #7A3C00;">Nazi</span> movement has left the leadership of the <span style="color: #D3C24D;">**[+ ddp_name +]**</span> and <span style="color: #C0A054;">**DVP**</span> convinced that their parties—and liberalism in general—are remnants of the past, and that they have a patriotic duty to die as quickly as possible so that a new, united bourgeois party can emerge from the ashes. [? if dvp_leader == "Dingeldey": Despite the present situation, the <span style="color: #C0A054;">**DVP**</span> still demands unity on their terms. ?] ?]{!</p>!}
{!<p class="other">!}[? if year = 1932 and month = 5 : The Austrian government has resigned due to the significant gains of the Austrian <span style="color: #7A3C00;">**NSDAP**</span> in recent local elections, leading to Engelbert Dollfuß being appointed chancellor by the President. Dollfuß now rules in a coalition with the <span style="color: #ABDDA4;">Heimwehr</span>. ?]{!</p>!}
{!<p class="other">!}[? if flavour_62_trigger and (flavour_62_trigger_time == time or (flavour_62_trigger_time == rubicon_time and rubicon)): Surprisingly, we've observed a slight thaw in our usually hostile relationship with the <span style="color: #700000;">**KPD**</span>. This shift stems from their leadership finally recognizing that the <span style="color: #7A3C00;">**NSDAP**</span> now poses the greatest threat to their survival. Increasingly, workers are coming to understand that <span style="color: #9B0000;">Marxist</span> unity is essential in the face of rising <span style="color: #7A3C00;">fascism</span>. ?]{!</p>!}
{!<p class="other">!}[? if papen_time == 1 and z_leader == "Kaas": Ludwig Kaas of the <span style="color: #000000;">Center Party</span> has announced his intention to resign as party chairman, citing ill health and proposing Heinrich Brüning as his successor. However, he has been persuaded to remain in office—on the condition that his deputy chairman, Joseph Joos, and Brüning handle most of the party’s day-to-day affairs. Kaas has since departed for an extended convalescent leave in South Tyrol. ?]{!</p>!}
{!<p class="other">!}[? if papen_time == 1 and not lvp_formed: The <span style="color: #D3C24D;">[+ ddp_name +]</span> has announced its firm opposition to Papen's cabinet of barons, and is attempting to mobilize the anti-Papen bourgeois electorate behind its own party, which is no longer burdened by the responsibilities of government. [? if dvp_exist and dvp_leader == "Dingeldey": Meanwhile, the <span style="color: #C0A054;">**DVP**</span> has been more accommodating toward the new government, despite its aristocratic appearance and their criticism that the <span style="color: #7A3C00;">**NSDAP**</span> is not included. Nonetheless, they view it as an improvement over the previous administration and refrain from anti-government attacks. This will greatly disrupt any attempts at liberal unity. ?][? if dvp_exist and dvp_leader != "Dingeldey": The <span style="color: #C0A054;">**DVP**</span> has also voiced strong opposition to the new government, criticizing its aristocratic composition and its widely unpopular decisions. This leaves Papen with an extremely narrow base of support in any future parliament. ?] ?]{!</p>!}
{!<p class="other">!}[? if papen_time == 2: Joseph Goebbels, the <span style="color: #7A3C00;">Nazi</span>'s propaganda mastermind has concluded after a few days of Papen's chancellorship, that the party must break with it in order to maintain popularity. The party can't support a government irreconcilably opposed by up to 90% of the German people. Their decision remains secret for now, and Papen continues his efforts to court their support. ?]{!</p>!}
{!<p class="other">!}[? if papen_time == 2 and not lvp_formed and not bourgeois_parliament and not bourgeois_list and dvp_exist and dvp_leader == "Dingeldey": Efforts toward bourgeois unity have stalled, with <span style="color: #D3C24D;">**[+ ddp_name +]**</span> refusing to work with the anti-democratic parties to their right. Not even Dietrich is willing to negotiate an arrangement between the bourgeois parties, who have drifted to the extreme right. [? if dvp_exist: Furthermore, the resignation of a <span style="color: #C0A054;">**DVP**</span> Reichstag member in protest against a united middle party has reinforced Dingeldey's determination to break negotiations with the <span style="color: #D3C24D;">**[+ ddp_name +]**</span>, and to seek an arrangement with the <span style="color: #3E88B3;">**DNVP**</span> instead. Furthermore, he refused to participate in any project that aims to combat the <span style="color: #7A3C00;">Nazi</span> movement. ?] The business community has since suspended all political donations until progress is made. ?]{!</p>!}
{!<p class="other">!}[? if papen_time == 3 and nsdap_r_prussia >= 25 and not in_far_right_coalition_prussia: Negotiations between the <span style="color: #000000;">Center Party</span> and the <span style="color: #7A3C00;">**NSDAP**</span> in Prussia have collapsed, driven by the former’s hostility toward its ex-member and current Chancellor Papen. ?]{!</p>!}
{!<p class="other">!}[? if papen_time == 4 and not lvp_formed and not bourgeois_parliament and not bourgeois_list and dvp_exist and dvp_leader == "Dingeldey": With Dingeldey pinning all of the blame on the <span style="color: #D3C24D;">**[+ ddp_name +]**</span> for the collapse of the negotiations, the <span style="color: #C0A054;">**DVP**</span>'s leadership has given him a blank check to explore an "alternative" arrangement with the <span style="color: #3E88B3;">nationalists</span>. ?]{!</p>!}
{!<p class="other">!}[? if papen_time == 5 and hugenberg_dvp: Out of all the bourgeois parties, the <span style="color: #D3C24D;">**[+ ddp_name +]**</span>, despite its critical lack of funds, stands the most resolute in its defense of the <span style="color: #000000;">We</span><span style="color: #DD0000;">im</span><span style="color: #FFCC00;">ar</span> Republic, even more so than the <span style="color: #000000;">Center</span> Party. Meanwhile, the <span style="color: #C0A054;">**DVP**</span> has been drawn closer to Papen, as dictated by the terms of their alliance. The lines between the two parties are increasingly blurred, and a <span style="color: #C0A054;">**DVP**</span> member even resigned in protest over Dingeldey's refusal to take the final step and merge with the <span style="color: #3E88B3;">**DNVP**</span> outright. ?]{!</p>!}
{!<p class="other">!}[? if papen_time == 6 and nsdap_r_prussia >= 25 and not in_far_right_coalition_prussia and spd_prussia and not presidential_powers and not prussian_reichsexekution: Rumors suggest Chancellor Papen has secured the support of his cabinet and President Hindenburg for a decree authorizing a <i>Reichsexekution</i> against Prussia, justifying it with a fabricated claim of a merger between the <span style="color: #c00000;">**SPD**</span> and <span style="color: #700000;">**KPD**</span>—a lie apparently convincing enough to sway Hindenburg. It seems all it takes is a pretext for him to present the already signed decree. ?]{!</p>!}
{!<p class="other">!}[? if papen_time == 7 and prussia_leader == "Papen": An immediate legal challenge has been launched against Papen's seizure of the Prussian government, with <span style="color: #c00000;">**SPD**</span> and <span style="color: #000000;">Center Party</span> officials requesting an interim injunction to block Papen and his appointed commissioners from assuming control. The <span style="color: #808080;">reactionary</span> court has rejected the injunction, and the main proceedings are scheduled to begin only after a three-month delay. ?]{!</p>!}
{!<p class="other">!}[? if papen_time == 7: Chancellor Papen has ordered the German delegation to walk out of the Geneva Disarmament Conference after <span style="color: #002654;">France</span> issued a strong objection to Germany’s demand for equal armament rights. Papen declared that Germany will not return to the conference unless the other powers agree to his terms. ?]{!</p>!}
{!<p class="other">!}[? if papen_time == 8 and prussia_leader == "Papen": The new right-wing and non-partisan Prussian cabinet assembled by <i>Reichskommissar</i> Papen has launched a large-scale purge of <span style="color: #c00000;">socialist</span> and <span style="color: #D3C24D;">democratic</span> officials from the civil service, forcing them into early retirement. Most of their replacements are conservatives—many of them <span style="color: #3E88B3;">nationalists</span>. The purge is particularly aimed at <span style="color: #c00000;">socialist</span> provincial presidents and police officials. An estimated 69 <span style="color: #000000;">rep</span><span style="color: #DD0000;">ubl</span><span style="color: #FFCC00;">ican</span> officials have been stripped of their power. Safe to say, the democratic bulwark has been dismantled. ?]{!</p>!}
{!<p class="other">!}[? if flavour_60_trigger and ((flavour_60_trigger_time == time and not rubicon) or (flavour_60_trigger_time == rubicon_time and rubicon)): The <span style="color: #D3C24D;">**[+ ddp_name +]**</span> performance, securing a minuscule [+ ddp_r +]% of the Reichstag due to its disadvantage from the lack of an electoral alliance, has sparked another round of debates over the party's continued existence. Resignation over the party's future is particularly strong within the business wing, with many members giving in to pressure to push for dissolution. [? if ddp_leader == "Dietrich": Even Dietrich has given up, announcing his resignation, but no one is willing to replace him.?] However, the party's active state organizations in their strongholds of Baden-Württemberg and Hamburg manage to persuade the party against dissolution. As a result, the party has adopted a more energetic tone, with power shared among Dietrich, the energetic Württemberg minister Reinhold Maier, and Hamburg Mayor Carl Petersen—the former chairman of the <span style="color: #D3C24D;">**DDP**</span>. ?]{!</p>!}
{!<p class="other">!}[? if year = 1932 and month = 8 and (week == 1 or not rubicon) and unemployed >= 25 : Perhaps the global recovery is finally on the horizon. Many economists now believe the slump has bottomed out, with the Frankfurt stock exchange climbing steadily—share and bond values have risen by over 30% since spring. But this resurgence remains abstract to the millions of unemployed Germans still trapped in poverty. ?]{!</p>!}
{!<p class="other">!}[? if papen_time == 9 and hitler_right_coalition >= 50: The aftermath of the Reichstag elections and the emergence of a Black-Brown majority has initiated negotiations between the <span style="color: #7A3C00;">**NSDAP**</span> and the <span style="color: #000000;">Center Party</span>. The latter hopes to restrain the <span style="color: #7A3C00;">Nazis</span> by compelling a potential Chancellor [+ nsdap_chancellor +] to govern through parliament rather than by decree. In doing so, he would be forced to consult a more conservative and dependable non-<span style="color: #7A3C00;">Nazi</span> cabinet—where real power would reside. ?]{!</p>!}
{!<p class="other">!}[? if papen_time == 9: General Schleicher has presented an enticing proposal to [+ nsdap_chancellor +], suggesting the formation of a presidential cabinet that would rule by decree. In this arrangement, Gregor Strasser would serve as Interior Minister of both Prussia and the Reich, while Schleicher believes he can control the <span style="color: #7A3C00;">Nazis</span> by taking the role of Defense Minister. However, the <span style="color: #7A3C00;">Nazi</span> leadership's enthusiasm quickly fades when President Hindenburg refuses to appoint [+ nsdap_chancellor +] as Chancellor. Papen, deeply offended by Schleicher's apparent willingness to abandon him, has used his growing rapport with Hindenburg to undermine Schleicher’s influence over the President. ?]{!</p>!}
{!<p class="other">!}[? if papen_time == 10: Following the results of the Reichstag elections, Papen and President Hindenburg met with [+ nsdap_chancellor +] to hear his demands for the party’s participation in government. [+ nsdap_chancellor +] requested to be appointed chancellor once again—a demand Hindenburg refused. Buoyed by their electoral triumph, the <span style="color: #7A3C00;">Nazis</span> have now officially reneged on their earlier promises to tolerate Papen. Going forward, they will pursue an all-or-nothing strategy: the chancellorship for [+ nsdap_chancellor +], or nothing at all. ?]{!</p>!}
{!<p class="other">!}[? if papen_time == 11: Papen has released the contents of last week's conversations with [+ nsdap_chancellor +] to the press, claiming that [+ nsdap_chancellor +] stormed into Hindenburg’s office to demand “total power over the state”—a violent one-party dictatorship—and was met with an angry rebuff from the president. This press release signals aims to signal the start of a counter-offensive against the <span style="color: #7A3C00;">Nazis</span>. But now, no party is willing—or able—to follow the chancellor into open confrontation with the extremists. Even the <span style="color: #000000;">Center Party</span> finds [+ nsdap_chancellor +]’s version of events more credible than Papen’s. ?]{!</p>!}
{!<p class="other">!}[? if papen_time == 11 and hitler_right_coalition >= 50: Coalition talks between the <span style="color: #000000;">Center Party</span> and <span style="color: #7A3C00;">**NSDAP**</span> have continued, putting pressure on Papen to outmaneuver his former party in gaining the favor of the <span style="color: #7A3C00;">Nazis</span>. Brüning, with permission from his party's leadership, has meet with the <span style="color: #7A3C00;">**NSDAP**</span>'s Strasser, promising support if he could convince [+ nsdap_chancellor +] to govern via a parliamentary cabinet with Schleicher as Defense Minister and Goerdeler as the commissioner of Prussia. ?]{!</p>!}
{!<p class="other">!}[? if papen_time == 11 and schleicher_kvp: Under pressure from their principal patron, General von Schleicher, the <span style="color: #0087DC;">**KVP**</span> is compelled to support the unpopular Papen government—or risk losing Schleicher’s covert Reichswehr funding. Outraged by the party's backing of such an anti-social chancellor, the <span style="color: #6D5298;">Christian Socials</span> resign their positions in protest. ?]{!</p>!}
{!<p class="other">!}[? if papen_time == 12 and hitler_right_coalition >= 50: A follow-up meeting to last week’s discussion about a potential Black-Brown alliance, set up by Strasser, has resulted in [+ nsdap_chancellor +] agreeing to delay the vote of no confidence against Papen. [+ nsdap_chancellor +] also appeared to agree with Brüning’s other demands. Closer cooperation is expected in the near future, if both parties are genuine in their commitment to govern together via parliament. [? if nsdap_chancellor == "Hitler": However, [+ nsdap_chancellor +] is a great liar. ?] ?]{!</p>!}
{!<p class="other">!}[? if year = 1932 and month = 8 and (week == 3 or not rubicon): Austria has obtained a League of Nations loan that is tied to a 20-year ban on Anschluss, but opposition from the <span style="color: #C8102E;">Social Democrats</span> and <span style="color: #0E428E;">Greater German Party</span> has stalled the law, before it finally is passed by a single vote. ?]{!</p>!}
{!<p class="other">!}[? if papen_time == 13 and hitler_right_coalition >= 50: The coalition talks between the <span style="color: #000000;">Center Party</span> and <span style="color: #7A3C00;">**NSDAP**</span> have been called off, as Brüning grows increasingly uneasy about the grave risks involved. This decision is also influenced by his friend and our party member, Hilferding, who argued that the <span style="color: #7A3C00;">**NSDAP**</span> would never restore parliamentary government. Hilferding also pointed out that many of Brüning's own party colleagues have fallen under <span style="color: #7A3C00;">Nazi</span> influence, a fact confirmed when they all but agreed to impeach President Hindenburg in the planned deal. Hilferding also remains the only one in our party to advocate for toleration of Papen's cabinet as the only means to prevent a <span style="color: #7A3C00;">Nazi</span> dictatorship. Brüning's goal is now to persuade the <span style="color: #7A3C00;">Nazis</span> to give the Papen government 'a fair chance' to revive the economy. ?]{!</p>!}
{!<p class="other">!}[? if papen_time == 15 and hitler_right_coalition >= 50: The <span style="color: #000000;">Center Party</span> and its grassroots supporters are becoming increasingly demoralized, largely due to Brüning’s semi-secret negotiations with the <span style="color: #7A3C00;">**NSDAP**</span>. Locally, party activists have faced the <span style="color: #7A3C00;">Nazis</span> as violent thugs, disrupting rallies and intimidating opponents—far from the image of a potential coalition partner. Some activists are even taking up arms in self-defense, losing faith in their party and its leadership. ?]{!</p>!}
{!<p class="other">!}[? if papen_time == 16 and papen_explode: Gregor Strasser of the <span style="color: #7A3C00;">**NSDAP**</span> has publicly opposed the party’s recent vote of no confidence and the snap elections it triggered, warning that such moves would only erode the <span style="color: #7A3C00;">Nazis’</span> popular support and squander their only real chance to seize power. In response, an unofficial faction of about sixty <span style="color: #7A3C00;">Nazi</span> Reichstag deputies has coalesced around Strasser—disillusioned by [+ nsdap_leader +]’s all-or-nothing approach. ?]{!</p>!}
{!<p class="other">!}[? if year = 1932 and month = 9 and (week == 3 or not rubicon) : General elections were held in Sweden, resulting in a great victory for Per Albin Hansson and his <span style="color: #D70926;">Social Democrats</span>, who now govern in a minority government. The <span style="color: #D70926;">Swedish Social Democrats</span> have embraced the concept of a 'people's home', a form of reformist socialism aimed at reducing class divides while working in the capitalist framework. Perhaps we should consider following their 'nordic' model? ?]{!</p>!}
{!<p class="other">!}[? if papen_time == 17 and prussia_leader == "Papen": The main proceedings in the case against Papen’s unconstitutional <i>Reichsexekution</i> of the Prussian government have begun, with a verdict expected in the coming weeks. Yet, the historically <span style="color: #808080;">reactionary</span> leanings of the judiciary place us at a clear disadvantage—despite support from the state governments of Bavaria and Baden, who have joined us in challenging the decree’s legality. ?]{!</p>!}
{!<p class="other">!}[? if year = 1932 and month = 10 and (week == 1 or not rubicon) : Chancellor of Austria Engelbert Dollfuß has used the "War Economy Enabling Act", a law passed during the days of the Austro-Hungarian Empire, which allowed him to restructure the Creditanstalt without parliamentary approval. While the <span style="color: #C8102E;">Austrian Social Democrats</span> have protested, Dollfuß' actions are technically legal. ?]{!</p>!}
{!<p class="other">!}[? if papen_time == 18 and papen_explode and not lvp_formed and ddp_ideology == "Right": The <span style="color: #D3C24D;">**[+ ddp_name +]**</span>, in an attempt to revive their poll numbers, have outlined the concept of "national democracy," aiming to rekindle the nationalistic impulse that played such a crucial role in the German liberal movement. However, it is essentially a restatement of classical liberal ideology, tailored to appeal to a more nationalistic German middle class. The national democracy program also reaffirms the <span style="color: #D3C24D;">**[+ ddp_name +]**</span>'s commitment to the <span style="color: #000000;">We</span><span style="color: #DD0000;">im</span><span style="color: #FFCC00;">ar</span> Republic and recommends constitutional changes to strengthen it. ?]{!</p>!}
{!<p class="dnvp">!}[? if papen_time == 18 and papen_explode and hugenberg_dvp and not lvp_formed: With its electoral alliance with the <span style="color: #3E88B3;">DNVP</span>, the <span style="color: #C0A054;">**DVP**</span>'s freedom of action has become increasingly constrained. Their rhetoric is aligning more closely with the <span style="color: #3E88B3;">**DNVP**</span>, now calling for the "liquidation of the <span style="color: #000000;">We</span><span style="color: #DD0000;">im</span><span style="color: #FFCC00;">ar</span> system," an authoritarian overhaul of its constitution, and the withdrawal of power away from the masses. ?]{!</p>!}
{!<p class="dnvp">!}[? if papen_time == 20 and papen_explode and hugenberg_dvp and not lvp_formed: Dingeldey and his associates are no longer content with merely renewing their electoral list connection with the <span style="color: #3E88B3;">**DNVP**</span>. Instead, they pushed for a unified Rechtsblock of the <span style="color: #C0A054;">**DVP**</span>, <span style="color: #3E88B3;">**DNVP**</span>, <span style="color: #3E88B3;">**Stahlhelm**</span>, and various industrialist and business organizations to provide cohesive support for Hindenburg and Papen. However, Hugenberg has shown utter indifference, refusing to commit to anything beyond a renewal of the electoral list connection. ?]{!</p>!}
{!<p class="other">!}[? if papen_time == 21 and papen_post_2: President Hindenburg has once again attempted to sound out the <span style="color: #7A3C00;">Nazis</span> after the recent election results, with [+ nsdap_chancellor +] signaling his readiness to seek the support of other parties if he were to be made chancellor first. Calling [+ nsdap_chancellor +]'s bluff, the president offered to appoint him, but only if he could secure a working majority in the Reichstag and allow the president to pick the defense and foreign ministers himself. As expected, [+ nsdap_chancellor +] declined Hindenburg's proposal and suggested instead that, with emergency powers, he could form a satisfactory government in no time. Convinced that [+ nsdap_chancellor +] sought nothing less than a presidential chancellorship, Hindenburg withdrew him from consideration. ?]{!</p>!}
{!<p class="other">!}[? if ((flavour_61_trigger_time == time and not rubicon) or (flavour_61_trigger_time == rubicon_time and rubicon)): Following another disastrous result for the <span style="color: #D3C24D;">**[+ ddp_name +]**</span>, a group of six former Reichstag deputies and high-ranking party members informed [+ ddp_leader +] that if a party congress—specifically meant to dissolve the party—isn't held, they would leave. However, this call has not gained much support, and Carl Petersen, former <span style="color: #D3C24D;">**DDP**</span> chairman, has used his mediation skills to prevent the dissidents from publicly announcing their break with the party. ?]{!</p>!}
{!<p class="other">!}[? if year = 1932 and month = 11 and (week == 1 or not rubicon): <span style="color: #00AEF3;">Democrat</span> Franklin D. Roosevelt has defeated incumbent <span style="color: #E81B23;">Republican</span> President Herbert Hoover in a landslide in the <span style="color: #B31942;">United</span> <span style="color: #0A3161;">States</span> presidential election. This is attributed by the Great Depression and Roosevelt's campaigning for a "New Deal", proposing expansive public works and government reform. ?]{!</p>!}
{!<p class="other">!}[? if papen_time == 22 and papen_post_2: Rumors are circulating widely that General Schleicher intends to undermine the very chancellor he helped install. Among his contacts, he's already being referred to as the chancellor-designate. ?]{!</p>!}
{!<p class="other">!}[? if papen_time == 23 and papen_post_2: President Hindenburg has commissioned [+ z_leader +] to make one final attempt at forming a majority coalition. Reporting back the next day, [+ z_leader +] confirmed that no such majority could be assembled. His recommendation was blunt: the only way forward is for the President to dismiss Papen, as the Reichstag will never accept him as chancellor. ?]{!</p>!}
{!<p class="other">!}[? if (papen_time or chancellor == "Schleicher") and year == 1932 and month == 12 and week == 1: The <span style="color: #7A3C00;">**NSDAP**</span> has suffered yet another crushing setback—this time in the local elections in Thuringia. The results show the party lost nearly a quarter of its vote share compared to the previous state election. While low turnout may be a factor, this and three other recent local elections have only reinforced the growing sense that the <span style="color: #7A3C00;">Nazis</span> have peaked and are rapidly losing support. ?]{!</p>!}
{!<p class="other">!}[? if schleicher_time == 0 and chancellor == "Schleicher": Despite his ousting, Papen remains in far closer physical proximity to the President than the chancellor does. At Hindenburg’s personal request, he continues to reside in an apartment within the Interior Ministry building—just steps from Hindenburg’s own quarters—while Schleicher lives miles away in a private residence. This arrangement has enabled Papen to scheme full-time, without attracting public attention. ?]{!</p>!}
{!<p class="other">!}[? if schleicher_time == 0 and chancellor == "Schleicher": Once a close ally, Oskar von Hindenburg has become an implacable enemy of the new chancellor—reportedly over a tasteless joke that wounded his pride beyond forgiveness. In Prussian times, such insults could end in duels; this time, the fallout may claim the life of the Republic. Two members of the Camarilla now harbor intense hostility toward Schleicher, and are working to sway the President into reconsidering his appointment. ?]{!</p>!}
{!<p class="other">!}[? if schleicher_time == 0 and chancellor == "Schleicher": Shortly after Schleicher’s appointment, the <span style="color: #0A3161;">Western</span> <span style="color: #C8102E;">Allies</span>, following protracted negotiations at the Geneva Disarmament Conference, conceded in principle to Germany’s right to equality in arms. The practical terms of this concession remain unclear, but in Schleicher’s view, it opens the path toward establishing an official militia service. ?]{!</p>!}
{!<p class="other">!}[? if schleicher_time == 1: During Schleicher's addresses to the nation, one central theme rings clear: "Create Work!" He has revived efforts to resettle the urban unemployed on rural homesteads carved from bankrupt estates in the sparsely populated East. Although the Reichstag remains dissolved and he cannot legislate, he has publicly opposed Papen’s changes to the unemployment insurance system, presenting himself as a proponent of neither <span style="color: #C0A054;">capitalism</span> nor <span style="color: #c00000;">socialism</span>. His ideological ambiguity has earned him the nickname "social general"—a label he embraces, claiming it aligns with the army’s tradition of solidarity between officers and soldiers. ?]{!</p>!}
{!<p class="dnvp">!}[? if schleicher_time == 1 and hugenberg_dvp: The <span style="color: #C0A054;">**DVP**</span>'s resurgence in the polls has led Dingeldey to become overconfident, unequivocally supporting Papen, then Schleicher, and even forming a parliamentary faction with the various splinter parties between it and the <span style="color: #3E88B3;">**DNVP**</span>. While this move has bolstered their influence, it has alienated Hugenberg and the <span style="color: #3E88B3;">**DNVP**</span>, on whom they still depend on as of their electoral alliance. ?]{!</p>!}
{!<p class="other">!}[? if schleicher_time == 2: For some odd reason, von Schleicher came to believe that nothing came of [+ nsdap_chancellor +]’s attempts to be appointed parliamentary chancellor simply because the man didn’t “at the bottom of his heart” want the office. By [+ month : month +], this suspicion had become a firm conviction; he repeatedly confided to journalists and his cabinet that the man simply doesn’t really want power. This belief is leading to Schleicher overestimating his chances of securing <span style="color: #7A3C00;">**NSDAP**</span> support for his cabinet—without [+ nsdap_chancellor +] as chancellor. ?]{!</p>!}
{!<p class="other">!}[? if schleicher_time == 2: Heavy electoral losses for the <span style="color: #7A3C00;">**NSDAP**</span> in local elections have further reinforced the disillusionment of the voters who have brought the movement to prominence, many who joined and donated to the party expecting to receive the spoils of victory are beginning to despair, withdrawing their donations. Admission fees for rallies, once a major source of income at the local level, have collapsed despite steep price reductions. [+ nsdap_chancellor +]'s failure to gain the chancellorship or enter government to constructively relieve Germany's economic woes has caused further frustration among the movement. However, <span style="color: #7A3C00;">Nazi</span> leaders are quick to suppress any leaks of dissent or rumors of decline to the public. ?]{!</p>!}
{!<p class="other">!}[? if schleicher_time == 3: The global economy is on the upswing—and with it, the erosion of the <span style="color: #7A3C00;">**NSDAP**</span> continues. The <span style="color: #000000;">rep</span><span style="color: #DD0000;">ubl</span><span style="color: #FFCC00;">ican</span> police force surveilling the <span style="color: #7A3C00;">**NSDAP**</span> in Munich has noted clear signs of disintegration. Resignations are occurring daily, membership dues arrive irregularly, and expulsions over indebtedness are increasingly common. Every branch of the party appears run down. As for morale, the police report observed: “The view that the peak has passed and favorable prospects are missed is commonly held among many members.” ?]{!</p>!}
{!<p class="other">!}[? if schleicher_time == 3: Schleicher still clings to the illusionary hope that the now powerless Strasser might somehow persuade [+ nsdap_chancellor +] to allow <span style="color: #7A3C00;">Nazi</span> participation in his cabinet, now that Strasser has returned from vacation. Strasser’s absence from the public eye has unsettled [+ nsdap_leader +], who now fears potential backroom plots to fracture the party—especially given the eagerness of Strasser’s remaining loyalists to do just that. In reality, all Strasser does is to warn former chancellor Brüning that Papen has arranged a secret meeting with Hitler—set to take place within a week or two—to conspire against their mutual enemy: Schleicher. ?]{!</p>!}
{!<p class="other">!}[? if schleicher_time == 4 and nazi_funds <= 1: The <span style="color: #7A3C00;">**NSDAP**</span>'s financial situation is becoming dire. Increasingly, equipment and supplies are being acquired on credit—credit that's already drying up under the weight of the party's existing debts. Tensions have begun to flare between the <span style="color: #7A3C00;">**SA**</span> and the party leadership over how to divide dwindling donations, with some members resorting to begging in the streets or organizing lotteries to scrape together funds. One more election, and the party could face a full-blown financial crisis. ?]{!</p>!}
{!<p class="other">!}[? if schleicher_time == 4: Despite promising President Hindenburg not to publicly criticize his favored former chancellor, Schleicher has done just that in a bid to distance himself from the deeply unpopular Papen. Hindenburg, displeased by the breach of trust, grows increasingly estranged from Schleicher. Papen, for his part, has shown little outward hostility toward his ouster—carefully masking his resentment and thirst for revenge beneath a façade of cordiality. In the meantime, Hindenburg has quietly authorized him to serve as his secret liaison to [+ nsdap_leader +]. ?]{!</p>!}
{!<p class="other">!}[? if schleicher_time == 4 and not schleicher_public_works_fast: Günther Gereke, the energetic special commissioner for public works, has grown frustrated with the chancellor after a request for intervention to clear bureaucratic obstacles was flatly rejected. His ambitious projects remain stalled. Gereke, disillusioned, has confided his disappointment to his close friend—none other than Oskar von Hindenburg. ?]{!</p>!}
{!<p class="other">!}[? if schleicher_time == 4 and schleicher_public_works_fast: Günther Gereke, the energetic special commissioner for public works, has had his project accelerated after his request for the chancellor to intervene and clear bureaucratic obstacles was approved. [? if schleicher_spd_r >= 4: Schleicher's eagerness to follow through on his promise to satisfy the unions and the <span style="color: #c00000;">**SPD**</span>, and to put additional pressure on the <span style="color: #7A3C00;">**NSDAP**</span> before their electoral base returns to work, has driven this decision. ?][? if schleicher_spd_r < 4 and workers_nsdap_normalized + workers_kpd_normalized >= 55: Schleicher's eagerness to fulfill his promise to the unions, and to curb the growing <span style="color: #700000;">**Communist**</span> vote share among radicalized workers, has motivated the move. ?][? if schleicher_spd_r < 4 and workers_nsdap_normalized + workers_kpd_normalized < 55 and unemployed >= 42: The disastrous state of the German economy has made rearmament a pipe dream, and Schleicher is eager to get it back on track. ?] ?]{!</p>!}
{!<p class="other">!}[? if (papen_time or chancellor == "Schleicher") and year == 1933 and month == 1 and week == 1: The campaign for the Landtag elections in the microscopically small Free State of Lippe has begun, with the <span style="color: #7A3C00;">Nazis</span> investing a substantial amount of resources to sway just a hundred thousand voters. However, despite its small size, a victory here could dispel the growing belief that the <span style="color: #7A3C00;">Nazis</span> are on the brink of a catastrophic decline. ?]{!</p>!}
{!<p class="other">!}[? if schleicher_time == 5: At a private dinner with journalists, Chancellor von Schleicher repeatedly expressed confidence that the <span style="color: #7A3C00;">Nazi</span> leader would eventually come around to tolerating his cabinet—despite [+ nsdap_leader +]’s public affirmations of an all-or-nothing course. This, even as Schleicher made clear he would dissolve the Reichstag if needed, a move he believes would cost the <span style="color: #7A3C00;">Nazis</span> further seats (though he seeks to avoid such a step, fearing the <span style="color: #700000;">**Communists**</span> would benefit most from their collapse). “They’ll soon be eating out of my hand,” were the chancellor’s exact words that evening. What Schleicher fails to grasp is that [+ nsdap_leader +] is no ordinary politician. He will not respond rationally, nor prudently, to Strasser’s break or to the <span style="color: #7A3C00;">**NSDAP**</span>’s electoral collapse. He believes destiny is on his side. ?]{!</p>!}
{!<p class="other">!}[? if schleicher_time == 5: Chancellor von Schleicher and his press secretary have deliberately “leaked” plans to appoint Strasser as Vice Chancellor, Interior Minister, and Minister-President of Prussia, alongside naming the <span style="color: #000000;">Center Party</span> labor leader Stegerwald as Labor Minister. The Agriculture and Economics Ministries were reportedly reserved for Hugenberg himself. It’s highly likely this is a bluff—an attempt to pressure the <span style="color: #7A3C00;">**NSDAP**</span> into dropping its total opposition, lest they risk being sidelined entirely. ?]{!</p>!}
{!<p class="dnvp">!}[? if schleicher_time == 5 and hugenberg_dvp: With Dingeldey's pretentiousness, fueled by the <span style="color: #C0A054;">**DVP**</span>'s uptick in polling, elevating it to the lead party on the "moderate" Right, Hugenberg is growing increasingly irritated by their independence and seeks to terminate their electoral alliance if new elections are called. While the <span style="color: #C0A054;">**DVP**</span> still hopes to renew the alliance, they begin exploring the possibility of an accommodation with the <span style="color: #D3C24D;">**[+ ddp_name +]**</span> and the bourgeois parties as a safeguard against near-certain annihilation if Hugenberg severs ties. Meanwhile, the DNV (German National Association), a non-partisan organization aiming to consolidate the German middle, is starting to repair its relations with the liberals after already securing the support of the non-liberal right, with politicians like Treviranus. ?]{!</p>!}
{!<p class="other">!}[? if schleicher_time == 6: Von Schleicher's bluffs—such as proposing a cabinet that includes both Stegerwald and Hugenberg—have lost all credibility. The <span style="color: #000000;">Center Party</span> has publicly ruled out Stegerwald serving alongside Hugenberg, a notorious enemy of organized labor. Moreover, the leak about the President’s meeting with Strasser has led to a permanent break between the <span style="color: #7A3C00;">Nazi</span> leader and Schleicher, ending any hope that Strasser might persuade him to support a cabinet involving Schleicher. His contradictory tactics and careless remarks have only deepened mistrust and furthered his political isolation. He only now condemns efforts to postpone elections—despite having supported the idea just a week earlier. ?]{!</p>!}
{!<p class="other">!}[? if schleicher_time == 6: With progress stalled across nearly all sectors, Schleicher has attempted to shift focus by bringing the issue of rearmament back into the public eye. He announced plans to establish a compulsory militia within a year, alongside a renewed commitment to arming the Reichswehr with heavy weaponry and instituting universal conscription. However, the impact of this intended public relations triumph is minimal, as news of Schleicher’s political setbacks dominates public attention. ?]{!</p>!}
{!<p class="dnvp">!}[? if schleicher_time == 6: Having failed to extract concessions from the chancellor to turn Germany into a dictatorship, the more radical elements within his party have pressured him into adopting an all-out opposition to Schleicher. A strongly worded resolution has been released, accusing him of "vacillation" in his willingness to "reform" the constitution. Furthermore, his initiative to restart peasant homesteads on bankrupt eastern estates has drawn the ire of conservative elites. With the next vote of no confidence looming, it may well come down to Schleicher versus the entire chamber. ?]{!</p>!}
{!<p class="other">!}[? if year = 1933 and month = 3 and not ((spd_in_government and foreign_minister_party = "SPD") or (austria_relation >= 1)) and (week == 1 or not rubicon): Dollfuß has abused his powers given in the "War Economy Enabling Act" to crack down on the workers in a railroad strike, which has resulted in all three presidents of parliament resigning amid a crucial vote. This crisis presented a perfect opportunity for Dollfuß to abolish the parliamentary system and restrict democratic rights, such as the freedom of the press and public protests. Another democracy has fell to authoritarianism. ?]{!</p>!}
{!<p class="other">!}[? if year = 1933 and month = 5 and (week == 4 or not rubicon) : Dollfuß's <span style="color: #7A3C00;">Austrofascist</span> dictatorship has banned the <span style="color: #700000;">Austrian Communists</span> and the paramilitary of the <span style="color: #C8102E;">Austrian Social Democrats</span>, the <span style="color: #C8102E;">Republican Protection League</span>. He has also established the <span style="color: #7A3C00;">Fatherland Front</span>, a party promoting a far-right <span style="color: #7A3C00;">fascist</span> and corporate ideology. ?]{!</p>!}
{!<p class="other">!}[? if year = 1933 and month = 11 and (week == 3 or not rubicon): The <span style="color: #EF1C27;">Spanish Socialists</span> and the <span style="color: #E25822;">Republican Left</span> have suffered an overwhelming defeat at the hands of the right wing <span style="color: #303180;">**CEDA**</span> and increasingly right-wing <span style="color: #A871A8;">Radicals</span>. ?]{!</p>!}
{!<p class="other">!}[? if year = 1934 and month = 2 and (week == 2 or not rubicon) and not austrian_parliament_seen: The Austrian Civil War has begun and ended in a matter of three days, fought between the <span style="color: #C8102E;">Republican Protection League</span> and Austria's <span style="color: #7A3C00;">Austrofascist</span> regime, resulting in a defeat for the <span style="color: #C8102E;">socialists</span> across the border. The uprising was sparked by efforts to suppress the remnants of the <span style="color: #C8102E;">Austrian Social Democrats</span> and their paramilitary wing. However, the poorly coordinated and weakened movement was swiftly crushed by the army. Perhaps things would have gone more differently if they have taken up arms earlier. ?]{!</p>!}
